"""Form 5 Economics (O Level 0525): Nature & Scope of Economics, Production, Population & Business Units, Demand, Supply & Price Mechanism (lessons).
Draft content: teaching figures are invented for practice; check against the Board's syllabus and your textbook."""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("economics", "form-5")

M1 = "Nature & Scope of Economics"
M2 = "Production, Population & Business Units"
M3 = "Demand, Supply & Price Mechanism"

LESSONS = []

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1. Scarcity, choice and opportunity cost
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Scarcity, choice and opportunity cost",
    title="Scarcity, choice and opportunity cost",
    slug="eco1-scarcity-choice-opportunity-cost",
    minutes=20,
    summary="Wants are unlimited but resources are limited, so every person, firm and government must choose. The opportunity cost of a choice is the next best alternative given up.",
    objectives=[
        "Define scarcity, want, need, choice and opportunity cost",
        "Explain why scarcity forces every economy to make choices",
        "Identify the opportunity cost of a decision from a list of alternatives",
        "Distinguish economic goods from free goods",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Scarcity", "Wants and needs", "Choice", "Opportunity cost", "Free goods and economic goods"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A student in Bamenda has FCFA 2 000 left in her pocket. She would like to top up her phone data, buy a notebook, take a taxi home "
          "and buy a snack. She cannot do all four. She must **choose**, and whatever she gives up is the price of her choice. "
          "That small problem is the whole of Economics in one picture."),
        D("**Economics** is the study of how individuals, firms and governments choose to use limited resources to satisfy unlimited wants.",
          title="Definition: economics"),
        H("Wants, needs and scarcity"),
        T("A **want** is anything a person would like to have. A **need** is something a person must have to live, such as food, water, clothing and shelter. "
          "Wants have no end: when one is satisfied, another appears. Resources (land, workers, machines, time and money) are limited."),
        D("**Scarcity** is the basic economic problem: there are not enough resources to produce everything people want. "
          "Scarcity exists in rich and poor countries alike.", title="Definition: scarcity"),
        KEY("Scarcity is not the same as poverty or shortage", "Students often mix these up.", cards=[
            ["Scarcity", "Resources are limited compared with wants. It affects everyone, always."],
            ["Shortage", "At the current price, buyers want more than sellers offer. It can be temporary."],
            ["Poverty", "A person or household has very little income or wealth."],
        ]),
        T("Because of scarcity, three questions must be answered by every society: **what** to produce, **how** to produce it, and **for whom** to produce it. "
          "Lesson 4 returns to these questions."),
        H("Free goods and economic goods"),
        D("A **free good** is available in unlimited amounts at no cost, such as air we breathe. An **economic good** is scarce and has a cost of production, "
          "so it has a price: plantain, a bag of cement, a haircut or a mobile phone.", title="Definition: free and economic goods"),
        T("Even water can be a free good in one place and an economic good in another. Rain falling on a village is free, but water treated, piped and delivered to a house in Douala has a cost, so it is an economic good."),
        H("Choice and opportunity cost"),
        T("Scarcity makes **choice** necessary. Every choice has a cost, and the real cost of anything is what you give up to get it."),
        D("**Opportunity cost** is the value of the **next best alternative** that is given up when a choice is made. "
          "It is not the total of all the alternatives, and it is not the money price.", title="Definition: opportunity cost"),
        EX("Worked example 1: a farmer chooses a crop", intro="A farmer near Kumba has one hectare. Teaching figures (invented for practice): growing cocoa would earn FCFA 300 000 a year, plantain FCFA 240 000 and maize FCFA 150 000. He chooses cocoa. What is his opportunity cost?", steps=[
            S("List the alternatives he gave up", text="Plantain (FCFA 240 000) and maize (FCFA 150 000)."),
            S("Pick the next best alternative", text="The best of the alternatives he gave up is plantain, at FCFA 240 000."),
            S("State the opportunity cost", text="His opportunity cost of growing cocoa is the FCFA 240 000 he could have earned from plantain. It is not 240 000 + 150 000 = 390 000."),
        ], check=r"\text{Opportunity cost} = 240\,000 \text{ FCFA (next best)}"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a student's evening", intro="After school, Mbah can (1) study for an examination, (2) play football with friends, or (3) help in his aunt's shop and be paid FCFA 1 500. He ranks them: study first, shop second, football third. He chooses to study. What does it cost him?", steps=[
            S("Identify the choice", text="He chooses to study."),
            S("Find the next best alternative", text="His second choice is helping in the shop."),
            S("State the opportunity cost", text="The opportunity cost of studying is the FCFA 1 500 (the shop work) he gave up. Football is not the cost because it was ranked lower."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Adding up every alternative", "Opportunity cost is only the single next best alternative forgone. Do not add all the options you gave up, and do not state the price paid as the opportunity cost."),
        MISTAKE("Saying scarcity only affects poor people", "Scarcity means limited resources relative to unlimited wants. A rich person and a rich country also have to choose because time, land and money are limited."),
        QUIZ("A government has money for either a new road or a new hospital, and ranks the hospital second. It builds the road. What is the opportunity cost of the road?",
             ["The cost of building the road", "The new hospital", "The new road and the new hospital together", "Nothing, because the road is built"], 1,
             "The opportunity cost is the next best alternative given up. The hospital was the second choice, so it is the opportunity cost of the road.",
             hint="Which alternative was ranked next best?",
             feedback=["That is the money cost. Opportunity cost is the best alternative given up.", "", "Only the next best alternative counts, not both.", "Every choice has an opportunity cost, even when money is spent well."]),
        QUIZ("Which statement about scarcity is correct?",
             ["It exists only in poor countries", "It means a country has no resources", "It means resources are limited compared with wants", "It disappears when prices fall"], 2,
             "Scarcity is the gap between limited resources and unlimited wants, so it exists everywhere.",
             hint="Compare resources with wants.",
             feedback=["Rich countries face scarcity too.", "Scarcity is not zero resources; it is not enough for all wants.", "", "Prices can change, but the limited supply of resources remains."]),
        TIP("Exam link: scarcity, choice and opportunity cost are examined in Paper 1 multiple choice and in Paper 2 essays on the nature of Economics. "
            "Questions often give a short situation and ask for the opportunity cost, or ask you to define scarcity. Write the definition first, then apply it."),
        PQ("Define scarcity and explain why it forces people to make choices.", "Scarcity is the limited supply of resources compared with unlimited wants. Because we cannot satisfy every want, we must choose which wants to satisfy and which to leave out. Every choice has an opportunity cost."),
        PQ("Aba has FCFA 5 000. She can spend it on a school bag (her first choice), a pair of sandals (second) or a meal at a restaurant (third). She buys the bag. State her opportunity cost.", "The sandals. They were her next best alternative, which she gave up."),
        PQ("Give two examples of economic goods and one example of a free good in a Cameroonian village.", "Economic goods: a bunch of plantain and a bag of cement (they are scarce and have a price). A free good: fresh air (not scarce and has no price)."),
        SUM("Scarcity means limited resources against unlimited wants. It affects every society.",
            "Because of scarcity we must choose; every choice involves giving something up.",
            "Opportunity cost is the next best alternative forgone, not the total of all alternatives.",
            "A free good is not scarce and has no price; an economic good is scarce and has a price."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2. The production possibility curve
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="The production possibility curve",
    title="The production possibility curve (PPC)",
    slug="eco1-production-possibility-curve",
    minutes=25,
    summary="The PPC shows the most of two goods an economy can produce with all its resources fully used. Points on it are efficient, inside it is waste, outside it is unattainable, and the curve shows opportunity cost.",
    objectives=[
        "Draw and interpret a production possibility curve from a schedule",
        "Calculate opportunity cost from a PPC table",
        "Explain what points on, inside and outside the PPC mean",
        "Explain what shifts the PPC outwards and why the curve bows outwards",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Production possibility curve", "Efficiency", "Unemployment of resources", "Increasing opportunity cost", "Economic growth"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Imagine a country that uses all its land, workers and machines to grow only two things: cocoa and plantain. "
          "If it puts more resources into cocoa, it has less for plantain. A **production possibility curve** (PPC) turns this trade-off into a graph."),
        D("A **production possibility curve** (also called a production possibility frontier) shows the maximum combinations of two goods "
          "that an economy can produce in a given time with all its resources fully and efficiently used and with technology unchanged.",
          title="Definition: production possibility curve"),
        T("The figures below are **invented for practice**. They are not real Cameroonian output."),
        CODE("""Point   Cocoa (thousand bags)   Plantain (thousand tonnes)
A              0                      100
B             10                       90
C             20                       70
D             30                       40
E             40                        0""", "text", title="Table 1. Teaching schedule (invented for practice)"),
        IMG("f5eco-1-ppc.svg", alt="A production possibility curve bowed outwards through points A to E, with point X inside the curve and point Y outside it",
            caption="Figure 1. The PPC from Table 1. Points A to E lie on the curve, X is inside it and Y is outside it."),
        H("Reading the curve"),
        KEY("What the position of a point tells us", "", cards=[
            ["On the curve (A to E)", "Possible and efficient: all resources are fully used. More of one good means less of the other."],
            ["Inside the curve (X)", "Possible but wasteful: resources are unemployed or badly used, for example idle farmland or workers without jobs."],
            ["Outside the curve (Y)", "Impossible for now: the economy does not have enough resources or technology. It becomes possible only if the curve shifts outwards."],
        ]),
        H("Opportunity cost on the PPC"),
        T("Moving along the curve shows opportunity cost. To get more cocoa, the economy must give up some plantain."),
        EX("Worked example 1: opportunity cost between points", intro="Use Table 1. What is the opportunity cost of producing 10 thousand extra bags of cocoa by moving from B to C?", steps=[
            S("Cocoa gained", text="From B to C, cocoa rises from 10 to 20, a gain of 10 thousand bags."),
            S("Plantain lost", text="Plantain falls from 90 to 70, a loss of 20 thousand tonnes."),
            S("Opportunity cost per unit", math=r"\frac{20}{10} = 2", text="Each extra thousand bags of cocoa costs 2 thousand tonnes of plantain."),
        ], check=r"90 - 70 = 20"),
        EX("Worked example 2: the cost rises as we move along", intro="Compare the opportunity cost of each extra 10 thousand bags of cocoa across the whole table.", steps=[
            S("A to B", text="Plantain falls by 10, so 1 tonne per bag."),
            S("B to C and C to D", text="Plantain falls by 20, then by 30. The costs are 2 and 3."),
            S("D to E", text="Plantain falls by 40, so the cost is 4."),
            S("Conclusion", text="The opportunity cost increases: 1, 2, 3, 4. This is why the curve bows outwards (is concave to the origin)."),
        ]),
        KEY("Why the PPC usually bows outwards", "Resources are not equally good at making both goods. At first the economy moves the resources best suited to cocoa. Later it must move resources that are better at plantain, so each extra bag of cocoa costs more plantain. This is called **increasing opportunity cost**. If resources were equally good at both goods, the PPC would be a straight line."),
        H("Shifts of the PPC"),
        IMG("f5eco-1-ppc-shift.svg", alt="Two production possibility curves, the second further from the origin; point C on the first moves to point C2 on the second",
            caption="Figure 2. Economic growth shifts the PPC outwards, so C2 becomes possible."),
        D("**Economic growth** is a rise in the productive capacity of an economy. On a PPC it is shown by an outward shift of the curve.", title="Definition: economic growth"),
        T("The curve shifts outwards when the quantity or quality of resources increases: new farmland or oil discovered, a larger workforce, better training and education, "
          "more machines (investment) and improved technology. It shifts inwards if resources are lost, for example by war, a flood or the loss of workers through disease."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing a movement along the PPC with a shift", "Moving from B to C is a **choice** between goods and does not change the economy's capacity. The curve shifts only when the economy's resources or technology change. Unemployment moves the economy inside the curve; it does not shift the curve."),
        MISTAKE("Saying a point outside the curve can be reached with better planning", "A point outside the PPC cannot be reached with today's resources and technology, however well the economy is managed. Only growth (an outward shift) makes it possible."),
        QUIZ("An economy is at point X inside its PPC. Which is the best explanation?",
             ["It is producing the maximum possible output", "Some resources are unemployed or badly used", "The economy has no scarcity", "The PPC has shifted outwards"], 1,
             "A point inside the curve means the economy could produce more of both goods, so resources are idle or inefficiently used.",
             hint="Compare the point with what is possible on the curve.",
             feedback=["Maximum output is on the curve, not inside it.", "", "Scarcity is present everywhere, including inside the curve.", "An outward shift would move the whole curve, not leave a point inside it."]),
        TIP("Exam link: PPC questions appear in Paper 1 (reading points and opportunity cost) and sometimes as part of a Paper 2 essay on scarcity and choice. Always label axes in diagrams and state what a shift means."),
        PQ("Using Table 1, calculate the opportunity cost of moving from D to E, in tonnes of plantain per bag of cocoa.", "Cocoa rises by 10 thousand bags (30 to 40). Plantain falls by 40 thousand tonnes (40 to 0). Opportunity cost = 40 / 10 = 4 thousand tonnes of plantain per thousand bags."),
        PQ("State two causes of an outward shift of a country's PPC.", "Any two: discovery of new resources, a growing and better-trained labour force, investment in machines (capital), improved technology."),
        PQ("Explain why unemployment of workers puts an economy inside its PPC.", "Unemployed workers are resources that are not used. With them idle the economy produces less of both goods than it could, so it lies inside the curve."),
        SUM("The PPC shows the maximum combinations of two goods an economy can produce when all resources are fully used.",
            "On the curve = efficient; inside = waste or unemployment; outside = not attainable yet.",
            "Opportunity cost is read from the table or curve: units of one good given up for each unit of the other gained.",
            "The curve bows outwards because opportunity cost increases. Economic growth shifts it outwards."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3. Positive and normative economics
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Positive and normative economics",
    title="Positive and normative statements",
    slug="eco1-positive-normative-statements",
    minutes=18,
    summary="A positive statement can be tested against facts; a normative statement is a value judgement about what ought to be. Economics uses both, but only positive statements can be proved true or false.",
    objectives=[
        "Define positive and normative economics",
        "Classify statements as positive or normative",
        "Explain why disagreements about normative statements cannot be settled by facts alone",
        "Give examples of each from everyday Cameroonian life",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Positive statement", "Normative statement", "Value judgement", "Testable"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Two friends discuss the price of rice. One says, 'The price of rice in the market has gone up this month.' The other says, 'The price of rice ought to be lower.' "
          "The first statement can be checked by asking traders and looking at receipts. The second is an opinion about what is good or fair. Economists call these two kinds of statements **positive** and **normative**."),
        D("**Positive economics** deals with facts and cause-and-effect: what **is**, **was** or **will be**. Its statements can be tested against evidence and shown to be true or false.", title="Definition: positive economics"),
        D("**Normative economics** deals with value judgements and opinions: what **ought to be** or **should be**. Its statements cannot be proved true or false by facts alone because they depend on a person's values.", title="Definition: normative economics"),
        KEY("Spot the difference", "Look for clue words.", cards=[
            ["Positive", "is, was, will, if...then, increased, caused, fell. It can be checked, even if it turns out wrong."],
            ["Normative", "should, ought, better, worse, fair, unfair, too high, too low. It expresses an opinion."],
        ]),
        H("Worked examples"),
        EX("Worked example 1: classify the statements", intro="Classify each statement as positive or normative: (a) The government should build more roads in rural areas. (b) When the price of plantain rises, the quantity demanded falls. (c) Unemployment among young people is the most serious problem in Cameroon. (d) A 10% rise in the price of fuel increases taxi fares.", steps=[
            S("(a) Look for clue words", text="'Should' shows a value judgement. It is **normative**."),
            S("(b) Can it be tested?", text="Yes, by collecting price and sales data. It is **positive**."),
            S("(c) 'The most serious' is a judgement", text="People may rank problems differently. It is **normative**."),
            S("(d) Cause and effect that can be checked", text="Taxi fares and fuel prices can be recorded. It is **positive** (even if the claim is wrong, it can be tested)."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: a positive statement can be false", intro="Is the statement 'Cocoa is the only crop grown in Cameroon' positive or normative? Is it true?", steps=[
            S("Classify", text="It states a fact claim that can be checked, so it is **positive**."),
            S("Test it", text="Cameroon grows many crops, including coffee, cotton, plantain, cassava and bananas. The statement is false."),
            S("Lesson", text="A positive statement is not always true. Positive means **testable**, not **correct**."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Thinking positive means good and normative means bad", "Positive and normative do not describe whether something is good. Positive means it can be tested against facts. Normative means it is an opinion about what ought to be."),
        MISTAKE("Calling a false fact a normative statement", "A false statement of fact is still positive because it can be tested. Only value judgements are normative."),
        H("Why the difference matters"),
        T("When people disagree about a positive statement, they can look at the evidence. When they disagree about a normative statement, they may agree on all the facts and still disagree, because they hold different values. "
          "For example, two people may agree that a tax on fuel would raise the price of transport (positive) but disagree about whether the government should introduce it (normative)."),
        T("Economists use positive economics to explain how the economy works and to predict the effects of policies. Governments and citizens then use normative judgements to decide which policy is best."),
        QUIZ("Which of the following is a normative statement?",
             ["Mobile money transfers rose in 2022", "Interest charged by microfinance institutions is too high", "A rise in income raises the demand for meat", "Cocoa prices fell last year"], 1,
             "'Too high' is a judgement about what is acceptable. It cannot be proved by facts alone.",
             hint="Which statement contains an opinion word such as 'too'?",
             feedback=["That is a fact that can be checked.", "", "That is cause and effect and can be tested.", "That is a fact that can be checked."]),
        QUIZ("The statement 'An increase in the minimum wage will reduce employment' is:",
             ["Normative, because it deals with wages", "Normative, because it might be wrong", "Positive, because it can be tested against evidence", "Neither, because it concerns the future"], 2,
             "It is a cause-and-effect claim that can be tested, even though it could be wrong.",
             hint="Can evidence be collected to check it?",
             feedback=["Topic does not decide the type.", "A false statement is still positive.", "", "Predictions about the future are still positive if they can be tested."]),
        TIP("Exam link: classifying statements as positive or normative is a common Paper 1 question and often opens a Paper 2 part on the scope of Economics. Always explain with a clue word or by saying 'it can be tested'."),
        PQ("Define normative economics and give one example.", "Normative economics deals with value judgements about what ought to be. Example: 'The government should reduce taxes on school materials.'"),
        PQ("Classify: 'When the price of bread rises, people buy less bread.' Give a reason.", "Positive. It is a statement of cause and effect that can be tested with data on prices and sales."),
        PQ("Explain why two economists may agree on the facts but disagree about a policy.", "The facts are positive but the policy choice is normative. They may value goals differently, such as equality versus growth, so they can reach different conclusions from the same facts."),
        SUM("Positive statements describe what is or will be and can be tested; they may be true or false.",
            "Normative statements express opinions about what ought to be; facts alone cannot settle them.",
            "Clue words such as should, ought, fair, too high point to normative statements.",
            "Economists use positive analysis to explain and predict, and normative judgements to choose policies."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3b. Measures of central tendency
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Positive and normative economics",
    title="Economic data: mean, median and mode",
    slug="eco1-mean-median-mode",
    minutes=22,
    summary="The mean, median and mode summarise a set of numbers with one typical value. Learn to calculate each from a list, a frequency table and grouped data, and to choose the best one.",
    objectives=[
        "Calculate the mean, median and mode of a list of values",
        "Calculate the mean from a frequency table and from grouped data",
        "Find the median and modal class of grouped data",
        "Choose the most suitable average and explain the effect of an extreme value",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Mean", "Median", "Mode", "Frequency table", "Grouped data", "Outlier"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Economists collect data on prices, incomes, outputs and populations. A long list of numbers is hard to use, so we summarise it with one typical value called an **average**. "
          "The Board's 2023 report lists measures of central tendency in its analysis of the Nature and Scope of Economics question, which most candidates who attempted it did not pass. "
          "They are worth practising because every mark can be earned by careful working."),
        D("The **mean** is the sum of all the values divided by the number of values.", title="Mean", formula=r"\text{Mean} = \frac{\text{sum of the values}}{\text{number of values}}"),
        D("The **median** is the middle value when the data are arranged in order. For an even number of values it is the mean of the two middle values.", title="Median"),
        D("The **mode** is the value that occurs most often. A set can have no mode, one mode or more than one.", title="Mode"),
        EX("Worked example 1: prices of tomatoes", intro="Seven stalls in a market sell a basket of tomatoes at these prices in FCFA (teaching figures, invented for practice): 400, 450, 500, 500, 550, 600, 1 800. Find the mean, median and mode.", steps=[
            S("Mean: add the values", math=r"400 + 450 + 500 + 500 + 550 + 600 + 1800 = 4800"),
            S("Divide by 7", math=r"\frac{4800}{7} \approx 685.7", text="The mean is about FCFA 686."),
            S("Median: the data are already in order", text="With 7 values the middle one is the 4th, which is 500."),
            S("Mode", text="500 appears twice and every other value once, so the mode is 500."),
        ], check=r"\text{Mean} \approx 686,\ \text{median} = 500,\ \text{mode} = 500"),
        KEY("Which average is best?", "The value 1 800 is an **outlier** (very different from the rest). It pulls the mean up to 686, while the median and mode stay at 500.", cards=[
            ["Mean", "Uses every value, but is pulled by extreme values. Good for symmetrical data."],
            ["Median", "Not affected by extremes. Good for incomes, house prices and skewed data."],
            ["Mode", "The most common value. Good for the most popular size or price."],
        ]),
        H("Frequency tables"),
        T("When values repeat, we list each value (x) with its frequency (f), the number of times it occurs. The mean is the sum of f times x divided by the sum of f."),
        EX("Worked example 2: customers at a roadside stall", intro="The number of customers who came in 20 different hours (teaching figures, invented for practice) is shown below. Find the mean, median and mode.", steps=[
            S("Set out the table", text="x = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 customers with frequencies f = 3, 5, 8, 3, 1. The total frequency is 3 + 5 + 8 + 3 + 1 = 20."),
            S("Find the sum of f x", math=r"3(1) + 5(2) + 8(3) + 3(4) + 1(5) = 3 + 10 + 24 + 12 + 5 = 54"),
            S("Mean", math=r"\frac{54}{20} = 2.7"),
            S("Median", text="With 20 values the median is the mean of the 10th and 11th. The running totals are 3, 8, 16, so both are in the group x = 3. The median is 3."),
            S("Mode", text="The highest frequency is 8, so the mode is 3."),
        ]),
        H("Grouped data"),
        T("For data in class intervals we do not know each exact value, so we use the **midpoint** of each class as its value. The class with the highest frequency is the **modal class**."),
        EX("Worked example 3: monthly incomes of 20 traders", intro="Monthly incomes in thousands of FCFA (teaching figures, invented for practice): 0 to under 10 (4 traders), 10 to under 20 (6), 20 to under 30 (8), 30 to under 40 (2). Estimate the mean, find the modal class and estimate the median.", steps=[
            S("Midpoints", text="5, 15, 25, 35."),
            S("Sum of f x", math=r"4(5) + 6(15) + 8(25) + 2(35) = 20 + 90 + 200 + 70 = 380"),
            S("Estimated mean", math=r"\frac{380}{20} = 19", text="The mean is about FCFA 19 000."),
            S("Modal class", text="The highest frequency is 8, so the modal class is 20 to under 30."),
            S("Median: find the 10th value", text="The running totals are 4, 10, 18, 20. The 10th value is at the end of the class 10 to under 20, so the median is estimated as 20 (thousand FCFA)."),
        ], check=r"\frac{380}{20} = 19"),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting to order the data before finding the median", "Always arrange the values from smallest to largest first. Taking the middle of an unordered list gives the wrong answer."),
        MISTAKE("Dividing by the number of classes", "For frequency and grouped tables divide the sum of f x by the **total frequency** (the sum of f), not by the number of rows."),
        MISTAKE("Using a class boundary instead of the midpoint", "For the mean of grouped data use the midpoint of each class, for example 15 for the class 10 to under 20."),
        QUIZ("Daily sales of a shop, in FCFA: 3 000, 4 000, 4 000, 5 000, 9 000. The mode is:",
             ["4 000", "5 000", "5 000, because it is the middle", "9 000"], 0,
             "4 000 occurs twice, more often than any other value.",
             hint="Which value is repeated?",
             feedback=["", "5 000 is the median, not the mode.", "The middle value is the median.", "9 000 is the largest value, not the most common."]),
        QUIZ("A few very rich people live in a village. To describe the typical income, which average is least affected by them?",
             ["The mean", "The median", "The total", "The range"], 1,
             "The median is the middle value, so extreme high incomes do not change it much.",
             hint="Which average ignores the size of the extreme values?",
             feedback=["The mean is pulled up by the large incomes.", "", "The total is not an average.", "The range is a measure of spread, not an average."]),
        TIP("Exam link: averages are tested in Paper 1 and in Paper 2 parts on the scope of Economics (for example the Board's report mentions measures of central tendency). Show the working: the sum, the division and the result."),
        PQ("Find the mean of 12, 15, 18, 20, 25.", "Sum = 90, divided by 5 = 18."),
        PQ("Find the median of 8, 3, 9, 5, 4, 6.", "Order: 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9. Six values, so the median is the mean of the 3rd and 4th: (5 + 6) / 2 = 5.5."),
        PQ("Explain why the median is often better than the mean for describing household income.", "A few very high incomes pull the mean up, so it may be higher than most households earn. The median is the middle household and is not affected by the extremes."),
        SUM("Mean = sum of values divided by the number of values; median = middle value of ordered data; mode = most frequent value.",
            "For a frequency table, mean = sum of f x divided by total frequency.",
            "For grouped data use class midpoints; the modal class has the highest frequency.",
            "Extreme values affect the mean most; the median is more reliable for skewed data."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4. Economic systems
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Economic systems",
    title="Economic systems: traditional, market, planned and mixed",
    slug="eco1-economic-systems",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Every economic system must decide what, how and for whom to produce. Traditional, free market, planned and mixed economies answer these questions differently, each with advantages and disadvantages.",
    objectives=[
        "State the functions (basic questions) of an economic system",
        "Describe traditional, market, planned and mixed economies",
        "Give advantages and disadvantages of the market economy and the planned economy",
        "State the characteristics of a mixed economy and explain why most countries are mixed",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Economic system", "What, how and for whom", "Traditional economy", "Market economy", "Planned economy", "Mixed economy", "Price mechanism"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In a village in the Grassfields, families farm as their parents did, and the chief and elders help to decide how land is shared. "
          "In Douala, thousands of traders and buyers decide in the market what to sell and at what price. "
          "These are different ways of organising an economy. Each answers the same three questions."),
        D("An **economic system** is the way a society organises the production and distribution of goods and services. Its functions are to decide **what** to produce, **how** to produce it and **for whom** to produce it.", title="Definition: economic system"),
        KEY("The three basic questions", "Scarcity forces every system to answer them.", cards=[
            ["What to produce?", "Which goods and services, and in what quantities: more food or more schools?"],
            ["How to produce?", "Which resources and methods: many workers or machines? Which technology?"],
            ["For whom to produce?", "How output is shared among people: by income, need or custom."],
        ]),
        H("The four systems"),
        D("A **traditional economy** is based on custom and habit. Methods, jobs and sharing are inherited: subsistence farming, hunting, fishing and craft work. Output is small and often consumed by the family.", title="Traditional economy"),
        D("A **market (free enterprise) economy** is one in which private individuals and firms own resources, and what is produced is decided by consumers' spending and producers' search for profit through the **price mechanism**, with little government involvement.", title="Market economy"),
        D("A **planned (command) economy** is one in which the government owns most resources and a central planning body decides what, how and for whom to produce.", title="Planned economy"),
        D("A **mixed economy** combines a **private sector** (owned by individuals and firms) with a **public sector** (owned or controlled by the state). Both the price mechanism and government decisions help to allocate resources.", title="Mixed economy"),
        IMG("f5eco-1-systems.svg", alt="A line from fully market to fully planned with the traditional, market, mixed and planned economies placed along it by how much the state decides",
            caption="Figure 1. Who decides? From private choice to state control. Most real economies lie in the middle."),
        H("Market economy: strengths and weaknesses"),
        KEY("Advantages of a market economy", "", cards=[
            ["Efficiency", "Prices guide resources to where buyers want them; wasteful firms lose money."],
            ["Choice", "Consumers have a wide range of goods and may choose freely."],
            ["Incentives", "Profit and rewards encourage hard work, saving and innovation."],
            ["Responds quickly", "Prices and profits change without waiting for officials to decide."],
        ]),
        KEY("Disadvantages of a market economy", "", cards=[
            ["Inequality", "People with little income or property get few goods."],
            ["Neglected goods", "Firms may not provide public goods such as street lighting, roads or defence because they are hard to charge for."],
            ["Monopoly power", "A single seller can raise prices and limit choice."],
            ["Harmful side effects", "Pollution and overuse of resources may go unpaid for."],
        ]),
        H("Planned economy: strengths and weaknesses"),
        T("In a planned economy the state can direct resources to national goals, reduce income inequality and provide basic services for all. "
          "But officials cannot know the wishes of millions of people, so shortages and surpluses of some goods are common. There is less choice, less reward for effort and little pressure to improve or innovate, and decisions can be slow."),
        H("The mixed economy"),
        KEY("Characteristics of a mixed economy", "", cards=[
            ["Two sectors", "Private enterprises exist side by side with state-owned enterprises."],
            ["Price mechanism plus government", "Most goods are allocated by prices, while the government provides public goods and services."],
            ["Private property allowed", "People may own land, businesses and savings, within the law."],
            ["Regulation and taxation", "The state taxes, spends, makes laws and may control some prices and industries to protect people."],
        ]),
        T("Almost every country today, including Cameroon, is a mixed economy: it has private farms, shops, banks and transport firms, and public bodies running services such as roads, schools and some utilities. The balance differs from country to country and changes over time."),
        EX("Worked example: model answer", intro="Explain why a mixed economy is considered better than a pure market or a pure planned economy.", steps=[
            S("State the problem of each pure system", text="A pure market economy leaves out public goods and leaves the poor behind. A pure planned economy lacks choice and incentives."),
            S("Show how mixing helps", text="A mixed economy uses prices and private profit to give efficiency and choice, and uses the state to provide public goods, reduce inequality and control monopolies."),
            S("Give an example", text="Private traders sell plantain in the market, while the government builds and maintains public roads that all traders use."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying that a mixed economy has no price mechanism or that the market economy has no government at all", "In a market economy the government still makes laws and protects property. In a mixed economy both prices and government decisions allocate resources."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up planned economy with mixed economy", "Planned = the state decides most things. Mixed = private and public sectors both matter. The Board's 2023 report names the functions of an economic system, the characteristics of a mixed economy and the advantages of a market economy in its question analysis, and most candidates who attempted that question did not pass, so learn these carefully."),
        QUIZ("In which system do consumers' spending and prices decide what is produced?",
             ["Traditional economy", "Planned economy", "Market economy", "Subsistence economy"], 2,
             "In a market economy consumers' demand and the profit motive decide, through the price mechanism.",
             hint="Which system leaves decisions mostly to private buyers and sellers?",
             feedback=["Custom and habit decide in a traditional economy.", "A central planning body decides in a planned economy.", "", "Subsistence is a way of farming, not a system answering what, how and for whom through prices."]),
        QUIZ("Which is a characteristic of a mixed economy?",
             ["All property is owned by the state", "There are both private and public sectors", "Prices play no part", "There is no taxation"], 1,
             "A mixed economy has a private sector and a public sector.",
             hint="Think of the word 'mixed'.",
             feedback=["That describes a pure planned economy.", "", "Prices do play a part in a mixed economy.", "Governments tax to pay for the public sector."]),
        TIP("Exam link: economic systems appear in Paper 1 and as a Paper 2 essay topic: functions of an economic system, characteristics of a mixed economy and advantages of the market economy are the sort of parts that appear. Name each point, then explain it in a sentence."),
        PQ("State the three basic questions that every economic system must answer.", "What to produce, how to produce it and for whom to produce it."),
        PQ("Give two advantages and two disadvantages of a market economy.", "Advantages: efficiency from the price mechanism; wide consumer choice; incentives for innovation. Disadvantages: inequality of income; public goods may not be provided; monopolies; pollution and other side effects. (Any two of each.)"),
        PQ("Describe four characteristics of a mixed economy.", "Public and private sectors exist together; resources are allocated by both the price mechanism and government decisions; private property is allowed; the government provides public goods, collects taxes and regulates."),
        SUM("Every economic system must decide what, how and for whom to produce.",
            "Traditional systems use custom, market systems use prices, planned systems use state orders, mixed systems use both.",
            "The market gives efficiency, choice and incentives but can lead to inequality and neglect of public goods.",
            "Most countries, including Cameroon, are mixed economies."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5. Factors of production
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Factors of production",
    title="The four factors of production and their rewards",
    slug="eco1-factors-of-production",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Land, labour, capital and enterprise are combined to produce goods and services. Each earns a reward: rent, wages, interest and profit. Know their features and how they are mobile or immobile.",
    objectives=[
        "Define production and the four factors of production",
        "State the reward paid to each factor",
        "Describe the main features of land, labour, capital and enterprise",
        "Explain the mobility of the factors and the difference between fixed and working capital",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Production", "Land", "Labour", "Capital", "Enterprise", "Rent, wages, interest, profit", "Mobility"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A woman in Mbalmayo wants to sell fried plantain chips. She needs a plot or stall (land), her own and her helper's work (labour), a fryer, oil, plantain and a table (capital), "
          "and the courage and skill to organise it all and carry the risk (enterprise). These four inputs are the **factors of production**."),
        D("**Production** is the creation of goods and services that satisfy human wants. The resources used in production are called **factors of production**.", title="Definition: production"),
        KEY("The four factors and their rewards", "Each factor is paid for its contribution.", cards=[
            ["Land", "All natural resources: soil, forests, minerals, rivers, the sea, climate. Reward: **rent**."],
            ["Labour", "All human work, physical and mental. Reward: **wages or salaries**."],
            ["Capital", "Man-made goods used to produce other goods: machines, tools, buildings, roads. Reward: **interest**."],
            ["Enterprise", "The entrepreneur who organises the other factors, takes decisions and bears risk. Reward: **profit**."],
        ]),
        H("Land"),
        T("In Economics, land means every gift of nature, not just the ground. Cameroon's fertile volcanic soils around Mount Cameroon, its forests, its oil and its rivers are all land."),
        KEY("Features of land", "", cards=[
            ["Fixed in total supply", "We cannot increase the total area, although we can improve its use."],
            ["Geographically immobile", "A plot cannot be moved, although its use can change."],
            ["Differences in quality", "Soil, rainfall and position differ from place to place."],
            ["Gift of nature", "It is not produced by people, so it has no cost of production."],
        ]),
        H("Labour"),
        T("Labour covers any human effort, from a farmer's work to a teacher's or a doctor's. Its quality depends on health, skills and education: this is called **human capital**."),
        KEY("Features of labour", "", cards=[
            ["Human and cannot be separated from the worker", "The worker has to be present and has feelings, needs and rights."],
            ["Differences in skills", "Labour can be skilled, semi-skilled or unskilled."],
            ["Partly mobile", "Workers can move between jobs (occupational mobility) and places (geographical mobility), but family ties, cost, language and housing limit this."],
            ["Supply responds to wages", "Higher wages attract more workers to a job."],
        ]),
        H("Capital"),
        D("**Capital** is man-made goods used to produce other goods and services. It is not money alone: money buys capital, but it is the machine, truck or building that produces.", title="Definition: capital"),
        T("**Fixed capital** lasts for many years of production: a tractor, a mill, a workshop, a road. **Working (circulating) capital** is used up or turned over in production: raw materials, fuel and goods in stock. "
          "**Human capital** is the knowledge and skills of workers. Capital is created by **saving** and **investment**: people do not spend all their income and use part of it to build more capital."),
        H("Enterprise"),
        T("The **entrepreneur** has the idea, brings the other factors together, decides what and how to produce and takes the **risk** that the business may fail. "
          "A farmer who sets up a poultry farm in Bafoussam or a young person who starts a mobile money kiosk is an entrepreneur."),
        EX("Worked example 1: classify the inputs", intro="A baker in Yaounde starts a bakery. Classify each: (a) the building site, (b) the oven, (c) the bakers employed, (d) the owner who takes the risk, (e) the flour in stock.", steps=[
            S("(a) The site", text="Land (rent is paid for it)."),
            S("(b) The oven", text="Capital, fixed capital (it lasts many years)."),
            S("(c) The bakers", text="Labour (they receive wages)."),
            S("(d) The owner", text="Enterprise (the reward is profit)."),
            S("(e) The flour in stock", text="Capital, working capital (it is used up in production)."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: rewards", intro="A farmer rents a plot for FCFA 40 000 a year, pays workers FCFA 150 000, pays FCFA 30 000 interest on a loan and sells his crop for FCFA 400 000 (teaching figures, invented for practice). Other costs are FCFA 100 000. What profit does he make?", steps=[
            S("Add the payments to the other factors", math=r"40\,000 + 150\,000 + 30\,000 + 100\,000 = 320\,000"),
            S("Subtract from revenue", math=r"400\,000 - 320\,000 = 80\,000", text="Profit is the reward of the entrepreneur, the remainder after the other factors are paid."),
        ], check=r"400\,000 - 320\,000 = 80\,000"),
        MISTAKE("Calling money capital", "In Economics, capital is a real good such as a machine or a building. Money is a way of obtaining capital, not capital itself."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the rewards", "Rent belongs to land, wages to labour, interest to capital and profit to enterprise. A very common error is to give profit as the reward of capital."),
        QUIZ("A tailor's sewing machine is an example of:",
             ["Land", "Labour", "Capital", "Enterprise"], 2,
             "A machine is a man-made good used to produce other goods, so it is capital.",
             hint="Is it natural, human or man-made?",
             feedback=["Land is a natural resource.", "Labour is human effort.", "", "Enterprise is the organising and risk-taking role of the owner."]),
        QUIZ("Which factor of production earns profit?",
             ["Land", "Labour", "Capital", "Enterprise"], 3,
             "Profit is the reward for organising production and bearing risk, which is enterprise.",
             hint="Who takes the risk?",
             feedback=["Land earns rent.", "Labour earns wages.", "Capital earns interest.", ""]),
        TIP("Exam link: factors of production and their rewards are tested in Paper 1 and are the basis of Paper 2 questions on production and on business units. A one-line definition, a Cameroonian example and the reward make a complete answer."),
        PQ("Name the four factors of production and the reward of each.", "Land (rent), labour (wages), capital (interest), enterprise (profit)."),
        PQ("Distinguish between fixed capital and working capital, with an example of each.", "Fixed capital lasts many production periods, e.g. a tractor. Working capital is used up or turned over quickly, e.g. fertiliser or raw materials."),
        PQ("State two reasons why labour is not perfectly mobile.", "Family ties and attachment to a home area; the cost of moving and housing; lack of the needed skills; language or cultural barriers. (Any two.)"),
        SUM("Production uses four factors: land, labour, capital and enterprise.",
            "Their rewards are rent, wages, interest and profit.",
            "Capital is a man-made producer good, not money; it can be fixed or working capital.",
            "Land is fixed in supply and immobile; labour is human and partly mobile; the entrepreneur bears risk."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6. Division of labour
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Division of labour and scale of production",
    title="Division of labour (specialisation)",
    slug="eco1-division-of-labour",
    minutes=20,
    summary="Division of labour means splitting production into tasks, each done by a different worker. It raises output per worker, but it also has disadvantages, and it is limited by the size of the market.",
    objectives=[
        "Define division of labour and specialisation",
        "Calculate output per worker before and after division of labour",
        "State the advantages and disadvantages of division of labour",
        "Explain why the size of the market limits division of labour",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Division of labour", "Specialisation", "Output per worker", "Size of the market", "Interdependence"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Think of a shirt-making workshop in Bamenda. If one tailor measures, cuts, sews, adds buttons and irons every shirt, she will finish only a few a day. "
          "If five workers each do one part of the job, the workshop produces many more. This is **division of labour**."),
        D("**Division of labour** is the splitting of the production of a good into separate tasks, each done by a different worker. **Specialisation** is when a worker, firm, region or country concentrates on producing one thing.", title="Definition: division of labour"),
        T("The Scottish economist Adam Smith described how a pin factory could make far more pins each day when workers each did one small step instead of making whole pins alone. "
          "Division of labour is one of the oldest ideas in Economics."),
        EX("Worked example 1: output per worker", intro="A workshop (teaching figures, invented for practice) has 4 tailors. When each tailor makes whole shirts alone, each makes 2 shirts a day. When the 4 tailors divide the work into cutting, sewing, buttoning and ironing, the workshop makes 20 shirts a day. By how much does output per worker rise?", steps=[
            S("Output before", math=r"4 \times 2 = 8 \text{ shirts a day}"),
            S("Output per worker after", math=r"\frac{20}{4} = 5 \text{ shirts per worker}"),
            S("Compare", math=r"\frac{5}{2} = 2.5", text="Each worker now produces 2.5 times as much as before, a rise of 150%. Total output rose from 8 to 20 shirts."),
        ], check=r"\frac{5 - 2}{2} \times 100 = 150\%"),
        H("Advantages of division of labour"),
        KEY("Advantages", "", cards=[
            ["Higher output", "Workers become faster and more skilled at a single task through practice."],
            ["Saves time", "No time is lost moving from one task to another or changing tools."],
            ["Machines can be used", "Simple tasks can be mechanised, and machines raise output further."],
            ["Suits abilities", "Workers are given the task they do best."],
            ["Lower cost per unit", "More output from the same resources lowers costs and can lower prices."],
        ]),
        H("Disadvantages"),
        KEY("Disadvantages", "", cards=[
            ["Boredom", "Doing the same small task all day is dull and can lower quality and morale."],
            ["Loss of wider skills", "Workers may find it hard to change jobs if their task disappears."],
            ["Interdependence", "If one worker is absent or one stage is delayed, production stops."],
            ["Unemployment risk", "Machines doing simple tasks can replace workers."],
            ["Loss of pride in the finished product", "Workers do not see the whole result of their work."],
        ]),
        H("Limits: the size of the market"),
        T("Division of labour works only if there is enough demand to buy the larger output. A village shop with few customers cannot divide its tasks among ten workers, but a big supermarket in Douala can. "
          "So the extent of division of labour is limited by the **size of the market**. Better transport, money and trade enlarge the market. "
          "Other limits are the nature of the product (a hand-carved piece of art cannot be divided easily) and the workers' willingness to accept very narrow tasks."),
        T("Division of labour applies to regions and countries too: Cameroon's South-West and Littoral regions grow bananas and rubber, the North grows cotton, and the regions trade. "
          "This is called **regional specialisation** and leads to trade, which is why money, transport and trade are so important."),
        EX("Worked example 2: model answer", intro="Explain why a fish-smoking business in Limbe may divide labour among its workers.", steps=[
            S("State the tasks", text="Workers can specialise in cleaning, arranging the fish, tending the fire and packing."),
            S("Link to higher output", text="Each worker becomes quicker at one job and no time is lost changing tasks, so more fish are smoked each day."),
            S("Mention cost or limit", text="The extra output lowers the cost per fish. It works only if there are enough buyers (the market must be large enough)."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Listing only advantages", "A full answer includes both advantages and disadvantages. A 'discuss' or 'assess' question wants both sides and a conclusion."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing division of labour with specialisation by country", "Division of labour is among workers in one production process; international specialisation is among countries. They are linked ideas but not the same."),
        QUIZ("What limits the extent of division of labour most?",
             ["The age of workers", "The size of the market", "The colour of the product", "The weather"], 1,
             "If the market is small, the larger output cannot be sold, so it is not worthwhile to divide the tasks.",
             hint="Who must buy the extra output?",
             feedback=["Age is not the main limit.", "", "Colour is irrelevant.", "Weather affects farming but is not the main economic limit."]),
        QUIZ("A disadvantage of division of labour is that:",
             ["Output per worker falls", "Workers become bored by repeating a single task", "Machines cannot be used", "Production costs always rise"], 1,
             "Repeating a narrow task day after day leads to boredom and weaker morale.",
             hint="Think about how the worker feels.",
             feedback=["Output per worker usually rises.", "", "Machines are easier to use when tasks are simple.", "Costs per unit normally fall."]),
        TIP("Exam link: division of labour is tested in Paper 1 and in Paper 2 parts on production. Give three or four advantages and disadvantages, each as a short sentence with a reason."),
        PQ("Define division of labour.", "Division of labour is the splitting of production into separate tasks, each performed by a different worker."),
        PQ("A factory has 6 workers who together make 30 units a day. After dividing the tasks the output is 90 units a day. Calculate the output per worker before and after.", "Before: 30 / 6 = 5 units per worker. After: 90 / 6 = 15 units per worker. Output per worker has tripled."),
        PQ("State three advantages and two disadvantages of division of labour.", "Advantages: higher output; saves time; allows use of machines; lower costs. Disadvantages: boredom; workers are less adaptable; production stops if one stage fails. (Any three and two.)"),
        SUM("Division of labour splits production into tasks done by different workers.",
            "It raises output per worker, saves time, allows machinery and lowers unit costs.",
            "Its disadvantages include boredom, loss of wider skills, interdependence and possible unemployment.",
            "The size of the market limits how far it can go."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 7. Scale of production
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Division of labour and scale of production",
    title="Scale of production: economies and diseconomies of scale and the growth of firms",
    slug="eco1-economies-of-scale-growth-of-firms",
    minutes=25,
    summary="When a firm grows its average cost can fall (internal economies of scale), and so can the costs of all firms in an industry (external economies). Too large a size can raise costs. Firms grow for many reasons.",
    objectives=[
        "Define scale of production, average cost, internal and external economies of scale",
        "Calculate average cost at different outputs",
        "Describe the types of internal economies and the external economies",
        "Explain diseconomies of scale and give reasons for the growth of firms",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Scale of production", "Average cost", "Internal economies of scale", "External economies of scale", "Diseconomies of scale", "Growth of firms"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A small bakery in Kumba bakes 200 loaves a day, using a small oven. A big bakery in Douala bakes 5 000 loaves a day with large ovens and bulk buying. "
          "The cost of each loaf is usually lower in the big bakery. This is the idea of **economies of scale**. The Board's 2023 report names internal and external economies of scale and the reasons for the growth of firms in its analysis of the Production question, and about four in five of the sampled candidates who attempted that question did not reach half marks."),
        D("**Scale of production** means the size of a firm's operations, shown by how much it produces and how many resources it uses.", title="Definition: scale of production"),
        D("**Average cost (cost per unit)** is total cost divided by the number of units produced.", title="Average cost", formula=r"\text{Average cost} = \frac{\text{Total cost}}{\text{Output}}"),
        EX("Worked example 1: average cost", intro="A firm making school bags (teaching figures, invented for practice) has a total cost of FCFA 500 000 when it makes 1 000 bags a month. After expansion it makes 4 000 bags at a total cost of FCFA 1 400 000. Find the average cost each time and the percentage change.", steps=[
            S("Average cost at 1 000 bags", math=r"\frac{500\,000}{1000} = 500 \text{ FCFA}"),
            S("Average cost at 4 000 bags", math=r"\frac{1\,400\,000}{4000} = 350 \text{ FCFA}"),
            S("Percentage fall", math=r"\frac{500 - 350}{500} \times 100 = 30\%", text="Cost per bag falls by 30%. Total cost rose but cost per unit fell: these are economies of scale."),
        ]),
        IMG("f5eco-1-lrac.svg", alt="U-shaped long-run average cost curve: cost per unit falls as output rises, reaches a lowest point and then rises again",
            caption="Figure 1. In the long run average cost falls through economies of scale, reaches a lowest point, then rises through diseconomies of scale."),
        H("Internal economies of scale"),
        D("**Internal economies of scale** are the cost advantages that come from the growth of a single firm itself, so that its average cost falls as its output rises.", title="Definition: internal economies of scale"),
        KEY("Types of internal economy", "", cards=[
            ["Technical", "Large firms can use big, efficient machines and divide labour. A large bakery's big oven bakes more loaves for each unit of fuel."],
            ["Managerial", "Large firms can employ specialist managers (accountants, marketing and production heads), who run the firm better."],
            ["Financial", "Large firms can borrow more easily and at lower interest, and can raise money by issuing shares."],
            ["Marketing (buying and selling)", "Buying in bulk earns discounts, and the cost of advertising is spread over many units."],
            ["Risk-bearing", "A large firm can spread risk by producing different products or selling in many markets."],
            ["Labour", "Large firms can train and attract the best workers and offer career paths."],
        ]),
        H("External economies of scale"),
        D("**External economies of scale** are cost advantages that come from the growth of the whole industry or area, not from one firm, and that lower the costs of every firm in it.", title="Definition: external economies of scale"),
        KEY("Examples of external economies", "", cards=[
            ["Pool of skilled labour", "Many firms in one area train and attract workers with the skills they all need."],
            ["Supporting industries", "Suppliers, repair services and transport firms set up nearby to serve the industry."],
            ["Shared infrastructure", "Better roads, power supply, banks and communications serve all firms."],
            ["Shared research and information", "Trade associations and shared knowledge reduce costs."],
        ]),
        T("A familiar example is the concentration of workshops and spare-part traders in a market district of a city: new mechanics find parts, customers and training in one place."),
        H("Diseconomies of scale"),
        D("**Diseconomies of scale** are the disadvantages of growing too big: average cost rises as output rises beyond a point.", title="Definition: diseconomies of scale"),
        T("Causes include communication problems and slow decisions in a big organisation, poor supervision and low morale, difficulty co-ordinating many departments, and strain on the local roads, power or labour market (external diseconomies such as congestion)."),
        EX("Worked example 2: classify the cost advantage", intro="Classify each as an internal or external economy: (a) a large firm buys flour at a bulk discount; (b) a technical college opens in a town and trains workers for all local firms; (c) a large bank gets a lower rate of interest on loans.", steps=[
            S("(a) Bulk buying", text="Internal (marketing/buying economy): it depends on the size of the firm."),
            S("(b) A technical college", text="External: it helps all firms in the area, not one."),
            S("(c) Lower interest", text="Internal (financial economy): it comes from the size and credit standing of the firm."),
        ]),
        H("Reasons for the growth of firms"),
        KEY("Why do firms grow?", "", cards=[
            ["To enjoy economies of scale", "Lower cost per unit and higher profit."],
            ["To gain a bigger market share", "More sales and more power over prices and suppliers."],
            ["To spread risk", "Diversification means a fall in one product does not ruin the firm."],
            ["Ambition and profit motive", "Owners want more profit and prestige; they use profits to expand (ploughed-back profits) or borrow."],
            ["To survive", "A small firm may be pushed out by large competitors, so it grows or merges."],
        ]),
        T("Firms grow **internally** by selling more and reinvesting profits, or **externally** by merging with or taking over other firms."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing internal and external economies", "Internal economies come from the firm's own size; external economies come from the size of the industry or area and help all firms. Ask: would a competitor in the same area also gain? If yes, it is external."),
        MISTAKE("Saying that bigger is always cheaper", "After a point, diseconomies of scale push the average cost up again. The best size is the one with the lowest average cost."),
        QUIZ("A firm gets cheaper loans because it is large. This is an example of a:",
             ["Technical economy", "Financial economy", "External economy", "Diseconomy of scale"], 1,
             "Banks lend more cheaply to large, safe firms. This is an internal financial economy.",
             hint="What is cheaper, and why?",
             feedback=["Technical economies come from machinery and production methods.", "", "It comes from the firm's own size, not from the industry.", "Costs fall, so it is an economy."]),
        QUIZ("Which of these is an external economy of scale?",
             ["A firm uses a larger machine", "A firm hires a specialist manager", "Many firms in a district draw on the same pool of trained workers", "A firm buys materials at a bulk discount"], 2,
             "A shared pool of skilled workers lowers costs for all firms in the area.",
             hint="Which one helps every firm in the district?",
             feedback=["Technical and internal.", "Managerial and internal.", "", "Marketing (buying) and internal."]),
        TIP("Exam link: economies of scale and the reasons for the growth of firms appear in Paper 1 and as a question in Paper 2 (the Board's report analyses internal and external economies and reasons for growth). Name the type, then explain how it lowers cost with a short example."),
        PQ("A firm's total cost is FCFA 900 000 for 3 000 units. When output rises to 10 000 units the total cost is FCFA 2 500 000. Calculate the average cost at each output.", "300 FCFA (900 000 / 3 000) and 250 FCFA (2 500 000 / 10 000). Average cost fell: economies of scale."),
        PQ("Explain two internal economies of scale.", "Technical: large machines and division of labour give more output per unit of input. Marketing: bulk buying brings discounts and advertising costs are spread over many units."),
        PQ("Give two reasons why firms seek to grow.", "To gain economies of scale and lower unit costs; to increase market share and profit; to spread risk. (Any two.)"),
        SUM("Average cost = total cost / output; economies of scale mean average cost falls as output rises.",
            "Internal economies come from the firm's own growth (technical, managerial, financial, marketing, risk-bearing, labour).",
            "External economies come from the growth of the industry or area and help all firms.",
            "Diseconomies make costs rise when a firm gets too big. Firms grow for economies of scale, market share, security and profit."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 8. Population theories
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Population theories, census and migration",
    title="Population theories: Malthus and the optimum population",
    slug="eco1-population-theories-malthus-optimum",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Malthus argued that population grows faster than food and is held back by checks. The optimum population gives the highest output per head; more than that is over-population, fewer is under-population.",
    objectives=[
        "State the main ideas of Malthus's theory and the positive and preventive checks",
        "Define optimum, over- and under-population",
        "Identify the symptoms and remedies of over- and under-population",
        "Explain criticisms of the Malthusian theory",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Malthusian theory", "Positive checks", "Preventive checks", "Optimum population", "Over-population", "Under-population"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Cameroon's population has grown in recent decades, and cities such as Douala and Yaounde have grown quickly. Is a bigger population good or bad for living standards? "
          "Economists have two main ways of thinking about this: the theory of **Thomas Malthus** and the idea of an **optimum population**. "
          "The Board's 2023 report names over-population and under-population in its population question, so we treat them carefully."),
        H("The Malthusian theory"),
        T("Thomas Malthus was an English clergyman and economist who published his essay on population in 1798. He argued that:"),
        KEY("Malthus's main ideas", "", cards=[
            ["Population grows geometrically", "Left unchecked it doubles each period: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16."],
            ["Food grows arithmetically", "Food supply increases by a fixed amount each period: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5."],
            ["The gap leads to checks", "Sooner or later population would exceed food supply, and checks would reduce it."],
        ]),
        IMG("f5eco-1-malthus.svg", alt="Line graph over five periods: population rises 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 while food rises 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, so the gap widens",
            caption="Figure 1. Malthus's argument (teaching figures): population doubles each period but food grows by a fixed step."),
        EX("Worked example 1: the widening gap", intro="Use Malthus's pattern. Population starts at 1 unit and doubles each period; food starts at 1 unit and rises by 1 each period. Compare them in period 5.", steps=[
            S("Population in period 5", math=r"1, 2, 4, 8, 16 \Rightarrow 16"),
            S("Food in period 5", math=r"1, 2, 3, 4, 5 \Rightarrow 5"),
            S("The gap", math=r"16 - 5 = 11", text="Population is more than three times the food supply. According to Malthus, checks must come into play."),
        ]),
        D("**Positive checks** are events that raise the death rate: famine, disease and war.", title="Positive checks"),
        D("**Preventive checks** are choices that lower the birth rate: delaying marriage (moral restraint) and having fewer children.", title="Preventive checks"),
        T("**Criticisms of Malthus:** farming technology, new seeds, fertilisers and machines raised food output far faster than he expected; international trade lets countries import food; "
          "birth rates fell in many countries as incomes and education rose, without famine; and in many places food grows geometrically too. "
          "His warning is still useful where land is scarce and yields are low."),
        H("The optimum population"),
        D("The **optimum population** is the size of population that, with the available resources and technology, gives the **highest output per head** (the best standard of living).", title="Definition: optimum population"),
        T("The optimum is not a fixed number. It changes whenever resources, skills or technology change. The figures below are **invented for practice**."),
        IMG("f5eco-1-optimum.svg", alt="Hump-shaped curve of output per head against population with the highest point labelled optimum; points to the left are under-populated and to the right over-populated",
            caption="Figure 2. Output per head rises, peaks at the optimum, and then falls (teaching figures)."),
        D("**Over-population** is when the population is larger than the optimum, so output per head falls: resources are too few for the number of people.", title="Definition: over-population"),
        D("**Under-population** is when the population is smaller than the optimum, so resources are not fully used and output per head could be raised by more people.", title="Definition: under-population"),
        KEY("Signs and remedies", "", cards=[
            ["Over-population signs", "Low output per head, unemployment and under-employment, pressure on land and housing, poor services, food shortages."],
            ["Over-population remedies", "Family planning and education, investment in jobs and technology, migration, better farming methods."],
            ["Under-population signs", "Unused land and resources, high wages but labour shortage, small markets and little division of labour."],
            ["Under-population remedies", "Encourage immigration and larger families, improve health to reduce deaths, train the workforce."],
        ]),
        T("Note that a country with a small population can be over-populated if its resources are even smaller, and a large country can be under-populated. The test is **output per head**, not population size alone."),
        EX("Worked example 2: identify the situation", intro="Output per head (invented for practice, in thousands of FCFA a year) is 62 at a population of 4 million, 100 at 8 million and 68 at 12 million. Classify the 4 million and 12 million situations.", steps=[
            S("Find the optimum", text="The highest output per head is 100 at 8 million, so the optimum is 8 million."),
            S("4 million", text="Below the optimum and output per head is lower (62): **under-populated**."),
            S("12 million", text="Above the optimum and output per head is lower (68): **over-populated**."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Defining over-population as 'too many people' only", "Over-population means more people than the optimum for the resources and technology, shown by falling output per head. It is not simply a large population."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing positive and preventive checks", "Positive checks raise deaths (famine, disease, war). Preventive checks reduce births (late marriage, birth control)."),
        QUIZ("According to Malthus, population grows:",
             ["Arithmetically", "Geometrically", "At the same rate as food", "Not at all"], 1,
             "Population doubles each period, a geometric increase; food grows by a fixed step.",
             hint="Compare 1, 2, 4, 8 with 1, 2, 3, 4.",
             feedback=["Arithmetic growth was his description of food supply.", "", "The difference in rates is his whole argument.", "He thought it grows unless checked."]),
        QUIZ("A country with fertile unused land and a shortage of workers is most likely:",
             ["Over-populated", "Under-populated", "At the optimum", "Having a Malthusian famine"], 1,
             "Unused resources and a labour shortage show it could produce more with a larger population.",
             hint="Are the resources fully used?",
             feedback=["Over-population means too many people for the resources.", "", "At the optimum output per head is at its highest.", "No sign of famine here."]),
        TIP("Exam link: over-population and under-population are named in the Board's 2023 analysis of the population question (the most popular question that year). Give a definition, the signs and a remedy, and always link to output per head and resources."),
        PQ("State Malthus's theory in two sentences.", "Malthus said population grows geometrically while food supply grows only arithmetically. Population would therefore outrun food, and checks such as famine, disease and war would bring it back."),
        PQ("Define optimum population and explain why it can change.", "It is the population that gives the highest output per head with given resources and technology. It changes with new resources, better skills and technology, because they change the output a given number of people can produce."),
        PQ("State two problems of over-population and two remedies.", "Problems: unemployment; shortage of land, food and housing. Remedies: family planning and education; creating jobs and improving technology. (Other correct points accepted.)"),
        SUM("Malthus: population grows geometrically, food arithmetically, so positive and preventive checks occur.",
            "Technology, trade and falling birth rates are the main criticisms of his theory.",
            "Optimum population gives the highest output per head with given resources and technology.",
            "Over-population: above the optimum; under-population: below it. The test is output per head."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 9. Census, migration and population measures
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Population theories, census and migration",
    title="Population change, the census and migration",
    slug="eco1-population-census-migration",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Population changes through births, deaths and migration. Learn to calculate birth and death rates, natural increase and net migration, to explain the census and age structure, and to describe the causes and effects of migration.",
    objectives=[
        "Calculate birth rate, death rate, natural increase and population growth",
        "Define census and explain why it is carried out",
        "Define emigration, immigration and net migration, and give causes and effects",
        "Explain growing and ageing populations and the dependency ratio",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Birth rate", "Death rate", "Natural increase", "Net migration", "Census", "Age structure", "Dependency ratio"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Every year some people in Kumba are born, some die, some arrive from villages and some leave for Douala or abroad. The total population changes through only three things: births, deaths and migration. "
          "The Board's 2023 report names birth and death rates, emigration and immigration, growing and ageing populations and the census as parts of its population question."),
        D("The **birth rate** is the number of live births per 1 000 of the population in one year. The **death rate** is the number of deaths per 1 000 of the population in one year.", title="Birth rate and death rate"),
        F(r"\text{Birth rate} = \frac{\text{births in a year}}{\text{total population}} \times 1000", title="Formula"),
        D("**Natural increase** is birth rate minus death rate. Dividing the answer by 10 gives the natural growth rate as a percentage. **Net migration** is immigrants minus emigrants. Population change = natural increase + net migration.", title="Natural increase and net migration"),
        EX("Worked example 1: rates for a town", intro="A town has 200 000 people (teaching figures, invented for practice). In one year there are 7 600 births and 2 400 deaths. Find the birth rate, the death rate and the natural growth rate.", steps=[
            S("Birth rate", math=r"\frac{7600}{200\,000} \times 1000 = 38 \text{ per 1 000}"),
            S("Death rate", math=r"\frac{2400}{200\,000} \times 1000 = 12 \text{ per 1 000}"),
            S("Natural increase", math=r"38 - 12 = 26 \text{ per 1 000}", text="As a percentage this is 26 / 10 = 2.6% a year."),
        ], check=r"7600 - 2400 = 5200 = 2.6\% \text{ of } 200\,000"),
        H("Migration"),
        D("**Migration** is the movement of people from one place to live in another. **Emigration** is leaving a country (or area) to live elsewhere; **immigration** is entering a country (or area) to live. **Net migration** = immigration minus emigration.", title="Definition: migration"),
        EX("Worked example 2: net migration and total growth", intro="In the same town 2 000 people move in and 5 000 move out during the year. Find the net migration and the actual change in population.", steps=[
            S("Net migration", math=r"2000 - 5000 = -3000", text="A net loss of 3 000 people."),
            S("Population change", math=r"5200 + (-3000) = 2200", text="The population rises by 2 200."),
            S("Growth rate", math=r"\frac{2200}{200\,000} \times 100 = 1.1\%"),
        ]),
        KEY("Causes of migration", "", cards=[
            ["Push factors (reasons to leave)", "Unemployment, poor services, land shortage, conflict or insecurity, drought or floods."],
            ["Pull factors (reasons to go)", "Jobs, better pay, schools, hospitals, safety, family or a better life."],
        ]),
        KEY("Effects of migration", "", cards=[
            ["On the place left", "Loss of young workers, fewer farmers; but money sent home (remittances) can help families."],
            ["On the place reached", "More workers and skills, bigger markets; but pressure on housing, water, schools, jobs and services."],
        ]),
        T("Rural-urban migration (from villages to cities) is very common in Cameroon. It can cause crowded neighbourhoods, unemployment and informal work in the cities."),
        H("The population census"),
        D("A **population census** is an official count of all the people in a country at a given time, together with information such as age, sex, occupation, education and housing. It is usually repeated about every ten years.", title="Definition: census"),
        T("A census is expensive and difficult, but governments need it to plan schools, hospitals, roads and food supply, to share public money among regions, to study the age structure, and to forecast future needs. Business and researchers also use the results. "
          "Problems include people who are hard to reach, mistrust, insecurity in some areas, errors and cost. Check the date of the last census in your textbook or from the National Institute of Statistics."),
        H("Growing and ageing populations"),
        T("A **growing (young) population** has a high birth rate and a large share of children. It needs schools and jobs for the young. An **ageing population** has a falling birth rate and a large share of older people, "
          "which means more spending on pensions and health care and fewer workers to pay taxes."),
        D("The **dependency ratio** compares dependants (people under 15 and over 64) with the working-age population (15 to 64).", title="Dependency ratio", formula=r"\text{Dependency ratio} = \frac{\text{under 15} + \text{65 and over}}{\text{15 to 64}} \times 100"),
        EX("Worked example 3: dependency ratio", intro="A town of 200 000 (teaching figures) has 80 000 people under 15, 112 000 aged 15 to 64 and 8 000 aged 65 and over. Find the dependency ratio.", steps=[
            S("Dependants", math=r"80\,000 + 8000 = 88\,000"),
            S("Divide by the working-age group", math=r"\frac{88\,000}{112\,000} \times 100 \approx 78.6", text="There are about 79 dependants for every 100 people of working age. A high ratio is a heavy burden on workers."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Dividing by 100 instead of 1 000", "Birth and death rates are per 1 000 of the population. Convert to a percentage by dividing by 10."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing emigration with immigration", "Emigrants exit, immigrants enter. A trick: E for exit (emigrate), I for in (immigrate)."),
        QUIZ("A country has 50 000 births and 20 000 deaths in a year, with a population of 1 million. The natural increase per 1 000 is:",
             ["30", "3", "70", "300"], 0,
             "Birth rate = 50 per 1 000, death rate = 20 per 1 000; natural increase = 50 - 20 = 30 per 1 000 (3%).",
             hint="Convert births and deaths to rates per 1 000 first.",
             feedback=["", "3 is the percentage, not the number per 1 000.", "You added births and deaths instead of subtracting.", "30 000 is the number of people; the rate is per 1 000."]),
        QUIZ("Which is a pull factor?",
             ["Drought on the farm", "Lack of jobs in the village", "Good schools and jobs in the city", "Conflict in the home area"], 2,
             "Pull factors attract migrants to the new place, such as jobs and schools.",
             hint="Does the factor attract people to the new place?",
             feedback=["A push factor.", "A push factor.", "", "A push factor."]),
        TIP("Exam link: birth and death rates, emigration and immigration, growing and ageing populations and the census appear in the Board's question analysis for Paper 2 population essays; the 2023 report shows this was the most popular question, so a good answer is valuable. Define each term, then give causes or effects."),
        PQ("In a village of 4 000 people there are 160 births and 40 deaths in a year. Calculate the natural increase per 1 000 (teaching figures).", "Birth rate = 160 / 4 000 x 1 000 = 40; death rate = 40 / 4 000 x 1 000 = 10; natural increase = 30 per 1 000 (3%)."),
        PQ("State three reasons why a census is carried out.", "To plan schools, hospitals and roads; to share public funds fairly; to know the age structure and forecast needs; to provide data for business and research. (Any three.)"),
        PQ("Explain two effects of rural-urban migration on the town that receives migrants.", "Pressure on housing, water and services; more unemployment and informal work; but a bigger labour force and market for local firms."),
        SUM("Birth rate and death rate are per 1 000; natural increase = birth rate - death rate; divide by 10 for a percentage.",
            "Net migration = immigrants - emigrants; population change = natural increase + net migration.",
            "A census is an official count of all people, used for planning, and is repeated about every ten years.",
            "Ageing populations raise the dependency ratio; young growing populations need jobs and schools."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 10. Business units 1
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Forms of business unit",
    title="Sole traders, partnerships and co-operatives",
    slug="eco1-sole-trader-partnership-cooperative",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Small and medium businesses come in several forms: sole proprietorships, partnerships and co-operative societies. Compare their ownership, capital, control, liability and advantages and disadvantages.",
    objectives=[
        "Define a business unit and describe a sole proprietor and a partnership",
        "State the advantages and disadvantages of each",
        "Explain the meaning of unlimited liability",
        "Describe a co-operative society and its principles",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Business unit", "Sole proprietor", "Partnership", "Unlimited liability", "Co-operative society"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Most Cameroonians who run a business start small: a woman sells tomatoes in Mokolo market, two friends open a mobile phone repair shop in Bafoussam, farmers join together to sell their cocoa. "
          "These are different **business units**: different ways of owning and running a business."),
        D("A **business unit** (or form of business organisation) is an organisation that produces goods or services. Its type depends on who owns it, how it is financed, who controls it and who bears the risks.", title="Definition: business unit"),
        H("The sole proprietorship"),
        D("A **sole proprietorship (sole trader)** is a business owned and controlled by one person, who provides the capital, takes the decisions, keeps the profit and bears the loss.", title="Definition: sole proprietorship"),
        KEY("Advantages", "", cards=[
            ["Easy to set up", "Few legal rules or costs."],
            ["Full control", "The owner decides quickly and keeps all the profit."],
            ["Personal service", "Close contact with customers, and flexible hours and prices."],
            ["Privacy", "Few details have to be published."],
        ]),
        KEY("Disadvantages", "", cards=[
            ["Unlimited liability", "The owner is personally responsible for all debts, even beyond the business assets."],
            ["Limited capital", "Savings and a loan are usually the only sources."],
            ["Heavy workload and risk", "One person does everything; sickness may close the business."],
            ["No continuity", "The business may end if the owner dies or retires."],
        ]),
        D("**Unlimited liability** means that the owner is responsible for **all** the debts of the business and may have to sell personal property to pay them.", title="Definition: unlimited liability"),
        H("The partnership"),
        D("A **partnership** is a business owned by two or more people (the partners) who agree to share the capital, work, profits and losses. Partnerships are usually governed by a **partnership agreement**. Check the legal limit on the number of partners in the rules applying in Cameroon.", title="Definition: partnership"),
        KEY("Advantages", "", cards=[
            ["More capital", "Several partners each contribute."],
            ["Shared skills and work", "Different skills; cover during absence."],
            ["Shared risk", "Losses are borne by more than one person."],
            ["Easy to form", "A simple agreement is enough."],
        ]),
        KEY("Disadvantages", "", cards=[
            ["Unlimited liability", "Partners are usually personally liable for the debts, and one partner can be made to pay all the debts."],
            ["Disagreements", "Partners may quarrel over decisions or sharing profits."],
            ["Limited capital", "Still smaller than a large company."],
            ["No separate identity", "The partnership may end if a partner dies or leaves."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: sharing profit", intro="Ngu and Fon are partners in a bakery (teaching figures, invented for practice). Ngu invested FCFA 3 000 000 and Fon FCFA 2 000 000. They agree to share profit in the ratio of their capital. The profit this year is FCFA 1 500 000. How much does each receive?", steps=[
            S("Find the ratio", text="3 000 000 : 2 000 000 = 3 : 2, so there are 5 parts."),
            S("Value of one part", math=r"\frac{1\,500\,000}{5} = 300\,000"),
            S("Ngu's and Fon's shares", math=r"3 \times 300\,000 = 900\,000 \quad ; \quad 2 \times 300\,000 = 600\,000"),
        ], check=r"900\,000 + 600\,000 = 1\,500\,000"),
        H("The co-operative society"),
        D("A **co-operative society** is a business owned and run by its members, who join together voluntarily to help themselves. Examples are co-operatives of cocoa or coffee farmers, credit unions and consumer co-operatives.", title="Definition: co-operative society"),
        KEY("Features", "", cards=[
            ["Voluntary and open membership", "Anyone who fits the purpose may join and leave."],
            ["One member, one vote", "Control does not depend on how many shares a member holds."],
            ["Members share the benefits", "Surplus may be returned to members or put into the business, often in proportion to their use of it."],
            ["Limited liability in many co-operatives", "Members risk only what they invested; check the rules for each type."],
        ]),
        T("Farmers' co-operatives can sell crops together to get better prices, buy inputs such as fertiliser in bulk, and share transport and storage. In Cameroon, co-operatives and common initiative groups are governed by laws that have been updated over time, so check the current rules."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking all small businesses have limited liability", "Sole traders and ordinary partners have unlimited liability. Limited liability belongs to companies (and some co-operatives)."),
        MISTAKE("Saying a sole proprietor has no workers", "A sole trader is the only **owner**; he or she may still employ workers."),
        QUIZ("Which business unit is owned by one person who keeps all the profit and bears all the losses?",
             ["Partnership", "Public limited company", "Sole proprietorship", "Co-operative society"], 2,
             "A sole proprietorship has one owner.",
             hint="Count the owners.",
             feedback=["Two or more owners.", "Owned by many shareholders.", "", "Owned by its members."]),
        QUIZ("In a co-operative society, voting is usually based on:",
             ["The number of shares held", "One member, one vote", "The age of members", "The profit each member makes"], 1,
             "Co-operatives are democratic: each member has one vote.",
             hint="Think of fair and equal control.",
             feedback=["That is how companies vote.", "", "Age does not decide votes.", "Profit does not decide votes."]),
        TIP("Exam link: forms of business unit are examined in Paper 1 and Paper 2, where the Board's 2023 analysis includes capital structure, the disadvantages of limited companies and shareholder questions (next lesson). For sole traders and partnerships, give a clear advantage and disadvantage with unlimited liability."),
        PQ("State two advantages and two disadvantages of a sole proprietorship.", "Advantages: easy to start; owner keeps all profit and has full control. Disadvantages: unlimited liability; limited capital. (Others accepted.)"),
        PQ("Two partners invest FCFA 800 000 and FCFA 1 200 000 and share profit in that ratio. Profit is FCFA 500 000. Find each share.", "Ratio 800 000 : 1 200 000 = 2 : 3, so 5 parts of 100 000. Shares: 200 000 and 300 000."),
        PQ("Explain why farmers may form a co-operative society.", "Together they can buy inputs in bulk at lower cost, sell crops in larger quantities at better prices, share transport and storage, and have a stronger voice."),
        SUM("A business unit is an organisation producing goods or services; types differ in ownership, finance, control and risk.",
            "Sole traders and partners have unlimited liability and limited capital.",
            "Partnerships add capital and skills but risk disagreements.",
            "Co-operatives are owned by members who vote equally and share benefits."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 11. Business units 2
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Forms of business unit",
    title="Limited companies, shares and public corporations",
    slug="eco1-limited-companies-shares-public-corporations",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Companies have limited liability and raise capital by issuing shares and debentures. Compare ordinary shareholders and debenture holders, understand a company's capital structure, and describe public corporations.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish private and public limited companies",
        "Explain limited liability and the capital structure of a company",
        "Compare ordinary shareholders and debenture holders and calculate dividends and interest",
        "State the advantages and disadvantages of limited companies and describe public corporations",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Limited liability", "Shares", "Ordinary shareholder", "Debenture holder", "Capital structure", "Dividend", "Public corporation"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A bakery growing from a sole trader to a chain of ten shops needs millions of francs, far more than one person can save. "
          "One answer is to form a **company**, which can collect money from many people. The Board's 2023 report names the capital structure of companies, ordinary shareholders and debenture holders, and the disadvantages of limited companies in its analysis of the business units question."),
        D("A **limited company** is a business that is a separate legal person from its owners, who are called **shareholders**. The shareholders have **limited liability**: they can lose only the money they put in, not their personal property.", title="Definition: limited company"),
        KEY("Two kinds of company", "In OHADA rules, applying to Cameroon, these are usually the SARL and the SA. Check the current rules.", cards=[
            ["Private limited company (Ltd)", "Shares are not offered to the general public and are sold only with agreement. It has few shareholders and the name usually ends in Ltd."],
            ["Public limited company (PLC)", "May offer shares to the public, and its shares may be traded on a stock exchange. It has many shareholders and must publish more information."],
        ]),
        H("Capital structure"),
        D("The **capital structure** of a company is the mix of long-term finance it uses: share capital (ordinary and preference shares), loan capital (debentures and long-term loans) and reserves (profits kept in the business).", title="Definition: capital structure"),
        KEY("The main sources", "", cards=[
            ["Ordinary shares", "Owners of the company. They get a **dividend** from profit, and the amount varies. They usually vote at meetings, and carry the most risk."],
            ["Preference shares", "Paid a fixed dividend before ordinary shareholders, usually no vote."],
            ["Debentures", "A long-term loan to the company, in return for **fixed interest**. Debenture holders are **creditors**, not owners."],
            ["Retained profits (reserves)", "Profits kept for growth. They belong to the shareholders."],
        ]),
        KEY("Ordinary shareholders compared with debenture holders", "", cards=[
            ["Position", "Ordinary shareholders are owners; debenture holders are lenders (creditors)."],
            ["Return", "Dividend from profit, which varies and may be nil; debenture interest is fixed and paid even if the company makes no profit."],
            ["Voting", "Ordinary shareholders usually vote; debenture holders do not."],
            ["Risk", "Ordinary shareholders are paid last and carry the highest risk; debenture holders are paid before them and often have the assets as security."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: interest first, then dividends", intro="A company (teaching figures, invented for practice) has 10 000 ordinary shares of FCFA 1 000 each and FCFA 4 000 000 of 8% debentures. Its profit before paying interest is FCFA 1 520 000. All the remaining profit is paid out as dividends. Find the interest, the dividend per share and the dividend rate.", steps=[
            S("Total capital raised", math=r"10\,000 \times 1000 + 4\,000\,000 = 14\,000\,000", text="Share capital is FCFA 10 000 000 and loan capital is FCFA 4 000 000."),
            S("Debenture interest", math=r"4\,000\,000 \times 8\% = 320\,000", text="This is paid first, as a fixed amount."),
            S("Profit left for shareholders", math=r"1\,520\,000 - 320\,000 = 1\,200\,000"),
            S("Dividend per share", math=r"\frac{1\,200\,000}{10\,000} = 120 \text{ FCFA}", text="This is a dividend of 12% on each FCFA 1 000 share."),
        ], check=r"120 \times 10\,000 = 1\,200\,000"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a bad year", intro="In a poor year profit before interest falls to FCFA 400 000, with the same capital. What happens to the interest and the dividend?", steps=[
            S("Interest is still due", text="Debenture interest of FCFA 320 000 must be paid, because it is a debt."),
            S("Left for shareholders", math=r"400\,000 - 320\,000 = 80\,000", text="Dividend per share = 80 000 / 10 000 = FCFA 8, only 0.8%."),
            S("Lesson", text="Shareholders carry the risk; debenture holders are paid first."),
        ]),
        H("Advantages and disadvantages of a limited company"),
        KEY("Advantages", "", cards=[
            ["Limited liability", "Shareholders can lose only what they invested."],
            ["Large capital", "Many shareholders can contribute."],
            ["Continuity", "It continues even when an owner dies or sells shares."],
            ["Economies of scale", "A large business can use specialists and large machines."],
        ]),
        KEY("Disadvantages", "", cards=[
            ["Cost and formalities", "Setting up and running are costly and need legal documents."],
            ["Publicity and regulation", "Accounts must be published and many rules apply."],
            ["Separation of ownership and control", "Directors and managers run the firm and may not act in the interests of all shareholders."],
            ["Impersonal", "Shareholders and workers may be less committed; decisions can be slow."],
            ["Share price risk (PLC)", "Shares may be sold to unwanted buyers or takeovers occur."],
        ]),
        H("Public corporations"),
        D("A **public corporation (public enterprise)** is a business owned by the state, set up by law to provide a service or product, and run by a board appointed by the government. Its aims are service to the public as well as profit.", title="Definition: public corporation"),
        T("In Cameroon, public enterprises have operated in areas such as water, electricity, ports, railways, telecommunications and plantations; check the current list and rules in your textbook, because some have been reorganised or sold (privatised). "
          "Reasons for having them include providing essential services to everyone, running industries that need huge capital, controlling natural monopolies and keeping key resources under national control. "
          "Problems often include poor management, political interference, losses and a lack of competition."),
        MISTAKE("Calling a debenture holder an owner", "Debenture holders lend money. They are creditors. Only shareholders are owners."),
        MISTAKE("Saying shareholders are always paid a fixed dividend", "Dividends of ordinary shares depend on profit and are decided by the directors. Only debenture interest and preference dividends are fixed."),
        QUIZ("Which statement about debenture holders is correct?",
             ["They are the owners of the company", "They vote at every meeting", "They receive a fixed rate of interest", "They are paid last if the company fails"], 2,
             "Debenture holders lend to the company and receive fixed interest.",
             hint="Are they owners or lenders?",
             feedback=["Owners are shareholders.", "Voting rights belong to ordinary shareholders.", "", "Debenture holders are creditors who are paid before shareholders."]),
        QUIZ("The main advantage of limited liability to a shareholder is that:",
             ["The company never fails", "A shareholder can lose only the money invested", "Dividends are guaranteed", "The shareholder controls the company alone"], 1,
             "Limited liability protects personal property: the shareholder risks only his or her investment.",
             hint="What can the shareholder lose?",
             feedback=["Companies can fail.", "", "Dividends depend on profit.", "Control is shared according to shares and voting."]),
        TIP("Exam link: the Board's 2023 analysis names the capital structure of companies, the disadvantages of limited companies and the comparison of shareholders and debenture holders. Use headings such as ownership, return, voting and risk to organise your answer."),
        PQ("State two differences between an ordinary shareholder and a debenture holder.", "Ordinary shareholders are owners and debenture holders are lenders; shareholders get a variable dividend, while debenture holders get fixed interest. (Voting and risk also accepted.)"),
        PQ("A company has FCFA 2 000 000 of 6% debentures. What interest does it pay each year?", "2 000 000 x 6 / 100 = FCFA 120 000."),
        PQ("Give three disadvantages of limited companies.", "Costly to set up; accounts must be published; separation of ownership and control; slow decisions. (Any three.)"),
        SUM("A limited company is a separate legal person; shareholders have limited liability.",
            "Capital structure = share capital, loan capital (debentures) and reserves.",
            "Ordinary shareholders are owners with variable dividends and the highest risk; debenture holders are lenders with fixed interest.",
            "Public corporations are state-owned and combine service and profit aims."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 12. Agriculture and industry in Cameroon
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Agriculture and industry in Cameroon",
    title="Agriculture and industry in Cameroon",
    slug="eco1-agriculture-industry-cameroon",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Agriculture employs many Cameroonians and produces cash and food crops. Industry adds value by processing. Both face problems that government and firms try to solve.",
    objectives=[
        "Name the main cash crops and food crops of Cameroon and where they grow",
        "Describe the contribution of agriculture to the economy",
        "State the problems of agriculture and industry and possible solutions",
        "Describe examples of agro-processing and other industries and the idea of industrialisation",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Cash crops", "Food crops", "Agro-processing", "Industrialisation", "Value added", "Primary, secondary, tertiary sectors"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Cameroon is often called 'Africa in miniature' because it has forests, savanna, mountains and coast. This variety lets it grow many crops. "
          "Agriculture is still the main activity of a large share of Cameroonians, and it feeds towns such as Douala, Yaounde and Bamenda. "
          "This lesson gives you general, well-known facts and teaching examples. Check the latest figures in your textbook or from official sources."),
        D("**Agriculture** is the growing of crops and rearing of animals (also forestry and fishing). It belongs to the **primary sector**. **Industry** (secondary sector) changes raw materials into finished goods and builds. **Services** form the tertiary sector.", title="The three sectors"),
        H("Cash crops and food crops"),
        KEY("Common examples", "", cards=[
            ["Cash crops (grown mainly for sale and export)", "Cocoa and coffee in forest and highland areas, bananas and rubber in the Littoral and South-West, oil palm, cotton in the North, tea in the South-West and North-West."],
            ["Food crops (grown mainly for eating and local sale)", "Plantain, cassava, maize, cocoyam, yams, beans, groundnuts, sorghum and millet in the north, rice."],
        ]),
        T("Many farmers grow both, selling any surplus in local markets. Most farms are small family farms using simple tools, with some large plantations such as those run by large companies and state-related bodies in the South-West."),
        H("Importance of agriculture"),
        KEY("Contributions", "", cards=[
            ["Food", "Feeds the population and reduces food imports."],
            ["Employment and income", "A major source of jobs, especially in rural areas."],
            ["Exports and foreign exchange", "Cocoa, coffee, bananas, rubber and cotton earn foreign currency."],
            ["Raw materials", "Supplies cocoa to chocolate makers, cotton to textile firms, oil palm to soap and cooking oil firms."],
            ["Markets", "Farm incomes buy goods from factories."],
        ]),
        H("Problems of agriculture"),
        KEY("Common problems and remedies", "", cards=[
            ["Poor roads and transport", "Crops rot before reaching markets. Remedy: build and maintain rural roads and storage."],
            ["Simple methods and low yields", "Hoes and cutlasses, little fertiliser. Remedy: training, better seeds, tools and extension services."],
            ["Lack of credit", "Banks lend little to farmers. Remedy: microfinance, co-operatives, farm credit schemes."],
            ["Price changes", "World prices of cocoa and coffee rise and fall, so incomes are unstable. Remedy: processing, diversification, stabilisation schemes."],
            ["Pests, disease and weather", "Remedy: research, irrigation and pest control."],
            ["Land problems and rural depopulation", "Disputes and young people leaving farms. Remedy: clear land rules, making farming profitable for young people."],
        ]),
        H("Industry"),
        T("Industrialisation means building factories and workshops to process raw materials. It adds value, creates jobs, saves foreign exchange by replacing imports and sells more valuable exports. "
          "Examples in Cameroon include food, drink and agro-processing (flour, beer and soft drinks, sugar, palm oil, cocoa and coffee processing), an aluminium smelter at Edea, an oil refinery at Limbe, cement, timber processing, soap, textiles and a growing small and medium-sized business sector. Check the latest information in your textbook."),
        EX("Worked example 1: value added", intro="A trader sells raw cocoa beans at FCFA 1 000 a kilogram (teaching figures, invented for practice). A small factory in Douala buys them and sells chocolate made from each kilogram of beans at FCFA 3 500. What is the value added by the factory (ignoring other costs)?", steps=[
            S("Final value", text="FCFA 3 500."),
            S("Subtract the value of materials", math=r"3500 - 1000 = 2500", text="The factory adds FCFA 2 500 per kilogram, which pays wages, energy, machines and profit."),
            S("Meaning", text="Processing locally keeps more of the value within the country, instead of exporting raw beans."),
        ]),
        KEY("Problems of industry", "", cards=[
            ["Power", "Electricity shortages and high costs."],
            ["Transport and infrastructure", "Poor roads and long delays at ports."],
            ["Small domestic market", "Low incomes limit demand."],
            ["Capital and skills", "Shortage of finance, technical skills and technology."],
            ["Competition from imports and smuggling", "Cheaper foreign goods can undercut local products."],
        ]),
        T("Possible remedies include investment in power, roads and the port of Douala and the deep-sea port at Kribi, training for workers, support for small and medium businesses, regional trade in CEMAC, and protection of local industries from unfair competition."),
        EX("Worked example 2: model answer", intro="Explain two ways in which developing agro-processing industries would help Cameroon.", steps=[
            S("Point 1: employment and income", text="Factories that process cocoa, coffee or palm oil give jobs to workers and give farmers a nearby buyer for their crops."),
            S("Point 2: more value and foreign exchange", text="Processed goods are worth more than raw crops, so exports earn more, and local products replace some imports."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Listing crops without location or use", "Name the crop and say whether it is grown for sale or food and where it is grown. 'Cocoa' alone earns little; 'cocoa, a cash crop grown in the forest zone and mostly exported' is stronger."),
        MISTAKE("Giving problems without remedies", "A common question asks for problems and solutions. For each problem state one practical solution."),
        QUIZ("Which of these is mainly a food crop?",
             ["Cocoa", "Plantain", "Rubber", "Cotton"], 1,
             "Plantain is grown mainly to be eaten and sold in local markets.",
             hint="Which one is the staple of many households?",
             feedback=["Cocoa is a cash crop for export.", "", "Rubber is a cash crop for export.", "Cotton is a cash crop."]),
        QUIZ("Processing cocoa beans into chocolate in Cameroon is an example of:",
             ["Primary production", "Agro-processing (secondary sector)", "A free good", "An import"], 1,
             "It changes a raw material into a finished product, which is secondary production.",
             hint="Is it growing a crop or changing a raw material?",
             feedback=["Growing the beans is primary.", "", "Chocolate is a produced good with a price.", "It is made locally, so it is not an import."]),
        TIP("Exam link: agriculture and industry questions may be asked in Paper 1 and Paper 2. Give factual, general points, avoid unsupported statistics, and always pair problems with remedies."),
        PQ("Name three cash crops and two food crops of Cameroon.", "Cash crops: cocoa, coffee, bananas (also rubber, cotton, oil palm). Food crops: plantain, cassava (also maize, cocoyam, yams)."),
        PQ("State three problems of agriculture in Cameroon and one remedy for each.", "Poor roads: build rural roads. Low yields: training and better seeds. Lack of credit: microfinance and farmers' co-operatives. (Others accepted.)"),
        PQ("Explain how industrialisation can reduce the need for imports.", "Local factories make goods that were imported, such as flour, soap or cement. People buy local goods, so less foreign currency is spent on imports."),
        SUM("Cameroon grows cash crops (cocoa, coffee, bananas, rubber, cotton) and food crops (plantain, cassava, maize).",
            "Agriculture gives food, jobs, exports, raw materials and markets.",
            "Its problems include poor roads, simple methods, lack of credit and unstable prices.",
            "Industry adds value, but needs power, transport, skills and finance; remedies include investment and training."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 13. Demand
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Demand",
    title="Demand: the law of demand, shifts and joint demand",
    slug="eco1-demand-law-shifts-joint",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Demand is the quantity people are willing and able to buy at each price. The demand curve slopes down. A price change moves along the curve; other factors shift it. Joint and competitive demand link goods.",
    objectives=[
        "Define demand and state the law of demand",
        "Read a demand schedule and draw a demand curve",
        "Distinguish a movement along the curve from a shift of the curve and list the factors that shift it",
        "Explain joint demand, competitive demand and the exceptions to the law of demand",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Demand", "Law of demand", "Demand schedule and curve", "Movement and shift", "Joint demand", "Competitive demand"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("When the price of plantain falls in a Bafoussam market, more people buy it and some buy larger bunches. When the price rises, some switch to cassava or rice. "
          "This everyday behaviour is the heart of **demand**."),
        D("**Demand** is the quantity of a good or service that consumers are **willing and able** to buy at each possible price, in a given period, other things being equal.", title="Definition: demand"),
        T("'Able' matters: a person who wants a car but has no money does not create demand. Demand is **effective demand**, backed by purchasing power."),
        D("The **law of demand** states that, other things remaining the same, as the price of a good rises the quantity demanded falls, and as the price falls the quantity demanded rises. Price and quantity demanded are **inversely** related.", title="The law of demand"),
        T("Why? A lower price lets existing buyers buy more, brings in new buyers who could not afford the good, and makes the good cheaper than its substitutes. The figures below are **teaching figures, invented for practice**."),
        CODE("""Price per bunch (FCFA)   Quantity demanded (bunches a day)
        200                          100
        400                           80
        600                           60
        800                           40
      1 000                           20""", "text", title="Table 1. A market demand schedule for plantain (invented for practice)"),
        IMG("f5eco-1-demand.svg", alt="Downward sloping demand curve D through five points from price 1000 and quantity 20 to price 200 and quantity 100; a and b mark two points",
            caption="Figure 1. The demand curve D from Table 1 slopes down from left to right."),
        H("Movement along the curve"),
        T("A change in the **price of the good itself** causes a **movement along** the demand curve. A fall in price from FCFA 800 to FCFA 400 moves us from point a (40 bunches) to point b (80 bunches). "
          "Economists call this an **extension** (price falls) or **contraction** (price rises) of demand, or simply a change in quantity demanded."),
        EX("Worked example 1: reading the schedule", intro="Use Table 1. By how much does the quantity demanded change when the price falls from FCFA 800 to FCFA 400?", steps=[
            S("Quantity at 800", text="40 bunches."),
            S("Quantity at 400", text="80 bunches."),
            S("Change", math=r"80 - 40 = 40", text="The quantity demanded rises by 40 bunches. This is a movement along the same curve."),
        ]),
        H("Shift of the curve"),
        T("When something **other than the price of the good** changes, the whole curve shifts. If demand **rises** the curve shifts **right** (more is bought at every price). If demand **falls** the curve shifts **left**."),
        IMG("f5eco-1-demand-shift.svg", alt="Three parallel demand curves: D0 in the middle, D1 to its right showing an increase in demand and D2 to its left showing a decrease",
            caption="Figure 2. A shift to the right (D1) is an increase in demand; a shift to the left (D2) is a decrease in demand."),
        KEY("Factors that shift demand (conditions of demand)", "Use the memory aid 'PITES': Price of related goods, Income, Tastes, Expectations, Size of population.", cards=[
            ["Income", "For a normal good, higher income raises demand (more meat, rice). For an inferior good, higher income lowers demand."],
            ["Prices of related goods", "A rise in the price of a substitute raises demand for this good. A rise in the price of a complement lowers it."],
            ["Tastes and fashion", "Advertising, culture and fashion change what people want."],
            ["Expectations", "If people expect prices to rise, they buy more now."],
            ["Population", "More people (or more people of the right age) mean more demand."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: classify the change", intro="State whether each causes a movement along the demand curve for plantain or a shift: (a) the price of plantain falls; (b) incomes in the town rise; (c) a health campaign encourages people to eat more plantain.", steps=[
            S("(a) Price of the good itself", text="Movement along the curve."),
            S("(b) Income rises", text="A shift to the right if plantain is a normal good."),
            S("(c) A change in tastes", text="A shift to the right."),
        ]),
        H("Joint and competitive demand"),
        D("**Joint demand (complementary demand)** is the demand for goods that are used together, so the demand for one depends on the demand for the other. Examples: a car and fuel, bread and butter, a phone and airtime, a pen and ink.", title="Definition: joint demand"),
        D("**Competitive demand (substitutes)** is the demand for goods that can replace one another. If the price of one rises, people switch to the other. Examples: rice and maize flour, tea and coffee, buses and taxis.", title="Definition: competitive demand"),
        T("For complements the price of one good and the demand for the other move in **opposite** directions: if fuel becomes more expensive, demand for cars falls. For substitutes they move in the **same** direction: if the price of rice rises, the demand for cassava rises."),
        T("**Composite demand** is demand for a good with several uses, such as timber for furniture, buildings and fuel. **Derived demand** is demand for something because it is needed to make another product, such as the demand for cocoa beans which comes from the demand for chocolate."),
        H("Exceptions to the law of demand"),
        T("In rare cases the demand curve may slope upwards: a **Giffen good** (an inferior staple whose price rises so much that poor consumers cannot afford better foods and buy more of it), a **luxury or status good** that people want because it is expensive, "
          "and when buyers **expect** the price to rise further. They are exceptions only."),
        MISTAKE("Saying a rise in price shifts the demand curve", "A change in the good's own price moves us **along** the curve. Only a change in income, tastes, related prices, expectations or population shifts the curve."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up 'a fall in demand' and 'a fall in quantity demanded'", "A fall in **demand** is a shift left, caused by something other than price. A fall in **quantity demanded** is a movement along the curve caused by a price rise. The Board's 2023 report names joint demand in its Price and Market question, the one most candidates failed, so learn complements and substitutes carefully."),
        QUIZ("Which will shift the demand curve for bread to the right?",
             ["A fall in the price of bread", "A rise in the price of butter, which is bought together with bread", "A rise in the price of a substitute such as cassava flour", "A rise in the price of bread"], 2,
             "If a substitute becomes dearer, people switch to bread, so demand for bread rises and shifts right.",
             hint="Which change is not a change in the price of bread itself, and raises demand?",
             feedback=["That moves us along the curve.", "A dearer complement lowers the demand for bread.", "", "That moves us along the curve (a contraction)."]),
        QUIZ("Petrol and cars are examples of:",
             ["Substitutes", "Complements (joint demand)", "Inferior goods", "Free goods"], 1,
             "They are used together, so demand for one depends on demand for the other.",
             hint="Are they used instead of each other or together?",
             feedback=["Substitutes replace each other.", "", "Inferior goods are bought less when income rises.", "Both are scarce and have a price."]),
        TIP("Exam link: demand is a core Paper 1 topic and a Paper 2 essay topic. Always state: the definition, the law, a schedule or diagram, then the shifting factors. Draw and label both axes (price and quantity)."),
        PQ("State the law of demand.", "Other things being equal, when the price of a good rises the quantity demanded falls, and when the price falls the quantity demanded rises."),
        PQ("Give two examples of joint demand and two of competitive demand.", "Joint: car and fuel; phone and airtime. Competitive: tea and coffee; rice and maize flour. (Other correct examples accepted.)"),
        PQ("A rise in income causes the demand for chicken to rise. Show this on a diagram in words.", "Draw price on the vertical axis and quantity on the horizontal. The demand curve D0 shifts to the right to D1: at every price a larger quantity is demanded."),
        SUM("Demand is the quantity people are willing and able to buy at each price; the curve slopes down.",
            "A change in the good's own price moves us along the curve; other factors (income, tastes, related prices, expectations, population) shift it.",
            "Complements (joint demand) are used together; substitutes (competitive demand) replace each other.",
            "Exceptions to the law, such as Giffen goods and status goods, are rare."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 14. Supply
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Supply",
    title="Supply: the law of supply, shifts, joint and competitive supply",
    slug="eco1-supply-law-shifts-joint",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at each price. The curve slopes up. A change in price moves along it; costs, technology, taxes and other factors shift it. Joint and competitive supply link products.",
    objectives=[
        "Define supply and state the law of supply",
        "Read a supply schedule and draw a supply curve",
        "List the factors that shift supply and distinguish a shift from a movement",
        "Explain joint supply and competitive supply with examples",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Supply", "Law of supply", "Supply schedule and curve", "Shift of supply", "Joint supply", "Competitive supply"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A farmer in Mbouda decides how much maize to bring to market. If the price is very low, he may keep most of it for his family. If the price is high, he sells more, and even grows more next season. "
          "That is the idea behind **supply**."),
        D("**Supply** is the quantity of a good or service that producers are **willing and able** to offer for sale at each possible price, in a given period, other things being equal.", title="Definition: supply"),
        D("The **law of supply** states that, other things remaining equal, as the price of a good rises the quantity supplied rises, and as the price falls the quantity supplied falls. Price and quantity supplied are **directly** related.", title="The law of supply"),
        T("Why? A higher price means higher profit, so existing firms produce more and new firms enter. In the short run costs per unit usually rise as output rises, so a higher price is needed to cover them. Teaching figures follow (**invented for practice**)."),
        CODE("""Price per bunch (FCFA)   Quantity supplied (bunches a day)
        200                          20
        400                          40
        600                          60
        800                          80
      1 000                         100""", "text", title="Table 1. A market supply schedule for plantain (invented for practice)"),
        IMG("f5eco-1-supply.svg", alt="Upward sloping supply curve S through five points from price 200 and quantity 20 to price 1000 and quantity 100",
            caption="Figure 1. The supply curve S from Table 1 slopes up from left to right."),
        EX("Worked example 1: reading supply", intro="Use Table 1. At what price will 60 bunches be supplied, and how many bunches are supplied at FCFA 1 000?", steps=[
            S("60 bunches", text="Find 60 in the quantity column: the price is FCFA 600."),
            S("At FCFA 1 000", text="The quantity supplied is 100 bunches."),
            S("Pattern", text="Each rise of FCFA 200 adds 20 bunches, so the quantity supplied is price / 10 (Q = 0.1P)."),
        ], check=r"Q = 0.1 \times 600 = 60"),
        H("Movement along and shift of supply"),
        T("A change in the **price of the good** moves us along the supply curve (an extension or contraction of supply). A change in any other condition shifts the curve: "
          "to the **right** if supply increases (more at every price) and to the **left** if supply decreases."),
        KEY("Factors that shift supply (conditions of supply)", "", cards=[
            ["Cost of production", "Higher wages, fertiliser or fuel costs reduce supply; lower costs increase it."],
            ["Technology", "Better methods or machines raise output and shift supply right."],
            ["Taxes and subsidies", "A tax on producers reduces supply; a subsidy increases it."],
            ["Weather and natural events", "Good rains raise farm supply; drought or floods cut it."],
            ["Prices of other goods and expected prices", "If another crop pays better, farmers switch; if a higher price is expected, sellers may hold goods back."],
            ["Number of producers", "More firms mean more supply."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: shifts in supply", intro="State the effect on the supply of plantain of (a) a drought, (b) a new variety of plantain that grows faster, (c) a rise in the price of plantain itself.", steps=[
            S("(a) Drought", text="Supply decreases: the curve shifts left."),
            S("(b) A faster-growing variety", text="Supply increases: the curve shifts right (better technology)."),
            S("(c) Price of plantain rises", text="Extension: a movement up along the same curve; no shift."),
        ]),
        H("Joint supply"),
        D("**Joint supply** occurs when producing one good automatically produces another, so the supply of both goods rises or falls together. These are called **by-products**.", title="Definition: joint supply"),
        T("Examples: beef and hides (leather), cotton lint and cottonseed (used for oil), palm oil and palm kernels, and sawmill timber and sawdust. "
          "If the price of beef rises and more cattle are slaughtered, the supply of hides also increases. The supply of the by-product depends on the supply of the main product."),
        H("Competitive supply"),
        D("**Competitive supply** occurs when the same resources can be used to produce either of two goods, so producing more of one means producing less of the other.", title="Definition: competitive supply"),
        T("Examples: land that can grow cocoa or plantain, a factory that can make either soap or candles, a taxi that can carry passengers or goods. "
          "If the price of cocoa rises, farmers shift land to cocoa, so the supply of plantain falls. For competitive supply the price of one good and the supply of the other move in **opposite** directions."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up joint demand and joint supply", "Joint demand is about goods **used together** by consumers (car and fuel). Joint supply is about goods **produced together** by the same process (beef and hides). Competitive supply is about alternative uses of the same resources. The Board's 2023 report names joint supply and competitive supply in its Price and Market question."),
        MISTAKE("Saying that a rise in the price shifts the supply curve", "A change in the good's own price is a movement along the curve. Only cost, technology, taxes, weather and other non-price factors shift it."),
        QUIZ("Which of these is an example of joint supply?",
             ["Tea and coffee", "Cars and petrol", "Beef and hides", "Land used for cocoa or maize"], 2,
             "Hides come from the same animals as beef, so they are produced together.",
             hint="Which pair is produced by one process?",
             feedback=["Substitutes in demand.", "Complements in demand.", "", "Competitive supply, not joint supply."]),
        QUIZ("Which will shift the supply curve of rice to the right?",
             ["A rise in the price of rice", "A new machine that cuts harvesting costs", "A rise in the cost of fertiliser", "A flood destroying fields"], 1,
             "Cheaper production due to better technology increases supply at every price.",
             hint="Which change lowers costs or raises output?",
             feedback=["That is a movement along the curve.", "", "That raises costs, so supply falls.", "That reduces supply."]),
        TIP("Exam link: supply, joint supply and competitive supply are the sort of terms examined in the price and market question; the Board's 2023 report shows the Price and Market question had the highest failure rate of the eight questions. Always give a definition and an example."),
        PQ("State the law of supply.", "Other things being equal, when the price of a good rises the quantity supplied rises, and when the price falls the quantity supplied falls."),
        PQ("Explain what is meant by joint supply, with two examples.", "Joint supply is when one production process gives two or more products. Examples: beef and hides; palm oil and palm kernels."),
        PQ("State three factors that can shift the supply curve to the left.", "A rise in production costs; a tax on producers; bad weather or drought; fewer firms. (Any three.)"),
        SUM("Supply is the quantity producers are willing and able to sell at each price; the curve slopes up.",
            "A change in the good's own price causes a movement along the curve; costs, technology, taxes, weather and other factors shift it.",
            "Joint supply: goods produced together (beef and hides). Competitive supply: alternative uses of the same resources (land for cocoa or plantain).",
            "Do not mix up complements in demand with joint supply."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 15. Market equilibrium
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Market equilibrium",
    title="Market equilibrium and changes in demand and supply",
    slug="eco1-market-equilibrium",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Equilibrium is where demand equals supply. At a higher price there is a surplus and at a lower price a shortage, and the price moves back to equilibrium. A shift of demand or supply gives a new equilibrium.",
    objectives=[
        "Define equilibrium price and quantity and find them from a table and a diagram",
        "Explain how surplus and shortage restore equilibrium",
        "Predict the effect of shifts in demand and supply on price and quantity",
        "Find equilibrium from simple linear equations",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Equilibrium price", "Equilibrium quantity", "Surplus (excess supply)", "Shortage (excess demand)", "Price mechanism"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In the market at Mokolo in Yaounde, tomato sellers who ask too high a price find tomatoes left unsold, so they lower the price. If tomatoes sell out in an hour, sellers raise their price the next day. "
          "Prices move until the amount buyers want matches the amount sellers want to sell. That point is the **equilibrium**."),
        D("**Market equilibrium** is the situation in which the quantity demanded equals the quantity supplied. The price at which this happens is the **equilibrium price** (market-clearing price), and the quantity bought and sold is the **equilibrium quantity**.", title="Definition: equilibrium"),
        CODE("""Price (FCFA)  Quantity demanded  Quantity supplied  Result
     200               100                20   Shortage 80
     400                80                40   Shortage 40
     600                60                60   EQUILIBRIUM
     800                40                80   Surplus 40
   1 000                20               100   Surplus 80""", "text", title="Table 1. Demand and supply of plantain (invented for practice)"),
        IMG("f5eco-1-equilibrium.svg", alt="Demand curve D falling and supply curve S rising crossing at E where price is 600 and quantity is 60; at price 800 a surplus bracket and at price 400 a shortage bracket",
            caption="Figure 1. Equilibrium at E: price FCFA 600, quantity 60 bunches. Above it there is a surplus; below it a shortage."),
        EX("Worked example 1: find equilibrium from the table", intro="Use Table 1. Find the equilibrium price and quantity and the market situation at FCFA 800.", steps=[
            S("Look for the row where demand = supply", text="At FCFA 600, 60 are demanded and 60 are supplied."),
            S("State equilibrium", text="Price FCFA 600, quantity 60 bunches."),
            S("At FCFA 800", math=r"80 - 40 = 40", text="Supply is 80 and demand is 40, so there is a surplus (excess supply) of 40 bunches."),
        ]),
        H("How the price mechanism restores equilibrium"),
        KEY("Surplus and shortage", "", cards=[
            ["Surplus (price above equilibrium)", "Sellers cannot sell all their goods and prices fall. As the price falls, quantity demanded rises and quantity supplied falls until the surplus disappears."],
            ["Shortage (price below equilibrium)", "Buyers cannot get all they want and bid the price up. As the price rises, quantity supplied rises and quantity demanded falls until the shortage disappears."],
        ]),
        T("This automatic adjustment is called the **price mechanism**. It also has three jobs: it **rations** scarce goods to those willing to pay, it **signals** to producers what consumers want, and it **gives incentives** to produce more where profits are high."),
        H("Changes in demand and supply"),
        T("When demand or supply shifts, the old equilibrium no longer holds and a new one is reached."),
        IMG("f5eco-1-eq-shift.svg", alt="Demand curve shifting right from D0 to D1 along an unchanged supply curve; equilibrium moves from E0 at price 600 quantity 60 to E1 at price 700 quantity 70",
            caption="Figure 2. An increase in demand (D0 to D1) raises both the price and the quantity."),
        EX("Worked example 2: an increase in demand from equations", intro="Demand is Qd = 120 - 0.1P and supply is Qs = 0.1P, with P in FCFA and Q in bunches (invented for practice). Find the equilibrium. Then find it again when demand rises to Qd = 140 - 0.1P.", steps=[
            S("Set Qd = Qs", math=r"120 - 0.1P = 0.1P"),
            S("Solve for P", math=r"120 = 0.2P \Rightarrow P = 600"),
            S("Find Q", math=r"Q = 0.1 \times 600 = 60"),
            S("After demand rises", math=r"140 - 0.1P = 0.1P \Rightarrow P = 700, \; Q = 70", text="Price and quantity both rise."),
        ], check=r"140 - 0.1(700) = 70 = 0.1(700)"),
        KEY("The four basic shifts (all else equal)", "Learn the pattern; the four results are always the same.", cards=[
            ["Demand increases", "Price rises, quantity rises."],
            ["Demand decreases", "Price falls, quantity falls."],
            ["Supply increases", "Price falls, quantity rises."],
            ["Supply decreases", "Price rises, quantity falls."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: supply falls", intro="A poor harvest cuts supply to Qs = 0.1P - 20, with demand unchanged at Qd = 120 - 0.1P. Find the new equilibrium and compare.", steps=[
            S("Set Qd = Qs", math=r"120 - 0.1P = 0.1P - 20"),
            S("Solve", math=r"140 = 0.2P \Rightarrow P = 700"),
            S("Quantity", math=r"Q = 120 - 0.1(700) = 50", text="Price rises from 600 to 700 and quantity falls from 60 to 50."),
        ], check=r"0.1(700) - 20 = 50"),
        MISTAKE("Stopping at the shift", "A diagram or answer must show the new equilibrium and state what happens to **both** price and quantity."),
        MISTAKE("Moving the wrong curve", "A change in tastes, income or population moves demand. A change in costs, technology or weather moves supply. Read the cause before you draw."),
        QUIZ("At a price above equilibrium there is:",
             ["A shortage", "A surplus", "Equilibrium", "No change"], 1,
             "Quantity supplied is greater than quantity demanded, so there is a surplus.",
             hint="Which is larger at a high price: what buyers want, or what sellers offer?",
             feedback=["A shortage occurs below equilibrium.", "", "Equilibrium is at one price only.", "Sellers lower prices to clear the surplus."]),
        QUIZ("A new machine lowers the cost of producing bread. What happens in the market for bread?",
             ["Price rises and quantity falls", "Price falls and quantity rises", "Price and quantity both rise", "Price and quantity both fall"], 1,
             "Supply increases (shifts right), so the price falls and the quantity bought and sold rises.",
             hint="Which curve moves, and which way?",
             feedback=["That is a fall in supply.", "", "That is an increase in demand.", "That is a fall in demand."]),
        TIP("Exam link: equilibrium is central to the Price and Market question in Paper 2 and appears in Paper 1. Draw clean diagrams, label D, S, P and Q, mark the equilibrium and describe the change in price and quantity."),
        PQ("Using Table 1, what happens at FCFA 400? How is the market restored to equilibrium?", "There is a shortage of 40 bunches (demand 80, supply 40). Buyers bid the price up, which raises quantity supplied and reduces quantity demanded until they are equal at FCFA 600."),
        PQ("Demand is Qd = 200 - 2P and supply Qs = 3P - 50 (invented for practice). Find equilibrium price and quantity.", "200 - 2P = 3P - 50 so 250 = 5P and P = 50. Q = 200 - 2(50) = 100."),
        PQ("Explain the effect on the price of cocoa of a rise in world demand, other things equal.", "Demand shifts right. At the old price there is a shortage, so the price rises and the quantity supplied rises, until a new higher equilibrium."),
        SUM("Equilibrium is where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied.",
            "Above it there is a surplus and the price falls; below it there is a shortage and the price rises.",
            "An increase in demand raises price and quantity; an increase in supply lowers price and raises quantity.",
            "For linear schedules set Qd = Qs and solve."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 16. PED
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Elasticity",
    title="Price elasticity of demand: measuring and using it",
    slug="eco1-price-elasticity-of-demand",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Price elasticity of demand measures how strongly the quantity demanded responds to a price change. Learn the formula, the percentage and midpoint methods, the types, the determinants and how it links to total revenue.",
    objectives=[
        "Define price elasticity of demand and calculate it by the percentage method and the midpoint method",
        "Classify demand as elastic, inelastic or unitary",
        "List the determinants of price elasticity of demand",
        "Use total revenue to see the effect of a price change on a firm's income",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Price elasticity of demand (PED)", "Elastic and inelastic", "Unit elasticity", "Midpoint method", "Determinants of PED", "Total revenue"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("If the price of salt in a Douala market rises by 10%, most people still buy almost the same amount, because salt is cheap and there is no substitute. "
          "If the price of one brand of mobile phone rises by 10%, many people switch to another brand and sales fall a lot. "
          "**Elasticity** measures this difference in responsiveness. The Board's 2023 report names the determinants of price elasticity of demand in its Price and Market question, the question with the highest failure rate that year."),
        D("**Price elasticity of demand (PED)** measures how much the quantity demanded of a good changes when its price changes.", title="Definition: PED",
          formula=r"PED = \frac{\%\ \text{change in quantity demanded}}{\%\ \text{change in price}}"),
        T("Because demand slopes downwards, PED is normally negative. We often ignore the sign and look at the size (the **absolute value**)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: percentage method", intro="The price of a litre of cooking oil rises from FCFA 500 to FCFA 600 and the quantity demanded falls from 80 to 60 litres a day (invented for practice). Calculate PED.", steps=[
            S("% change in price", math=r"\frac{600 - 500}{500} \times 100 = +20\%"),
            S("% change in quantity", math=r"\frac{60 - 80}{80} \times 100 = -25\%"),
            S("Divide", math=r"PED = \frac{-25}{+20} = -1.25", text="The size is 1.25, which is greater than 1, so demand is elastic."),
        ]),
        KEY("Types of PED (size, ignoring the sign)", "", cards=[
            ["Elastic: greater than 1", "Quantity changes by a bigger percentage than the price. Demand is responsive. The curve is relatively flat."],
            ["Inelastic: between 0 and 1", "Quantity changes by a smaller percentage than the price. Demand is unresponsive. The curve is relatively steep."],
            ["Unitary: equal to 1", "Quantity and price change by the same percentage."],
            ["Perfectly inelastic: 0", "Quantity does not change at all (for example, a life-saving drug)."],
        ]),
        IMG("f5eco-1-ped.svg", alt="Two demand curves side by side with the same fall in price: on the steep inelastic curve quantity changes little, on the flatter elastic curve quantity changes a lot",
            caption="Figure 1. The same price change causes a small change in quantity when demand is inelastic and a large change when it is elastic."),
        H("The midpoint method"),
        T("The percentage method gives different answers depending on whether the price rises or falls. The **midpoint (arc) method** uses the average of the old and new values as the base, so the answer is the same in both directions."),
        F(r"PED = \frac{\Delta Q / \text{average } Q}{\Delta P / \text{average } P}", title="Midpoint formula"),
        EX("Worked example 2: the same data by the midpoint method", intro="Use the cooking oil data: P from 500 to 600, Q from 80 to 60.", steps=[
            S("Average Q and average P", math=r"\frac{80+60}{2} = 70 \quad ; \quad \frac{500+600}{2} = 550"),
            S("% change in Q", math=r"\frac{-20}{70} \times 100 = -28.57\%"),
            S("% change in P", math=r"\frac{100}{550} \times 100 = +18.18\%"),
            S("Divide", math=r"PED = \frac{-28.57}{18.18} = -1.57", text="Size 1.57: demand is elastic. (The percentage method gave 1.25, the midpoint method gives 1.57. Use the method the question asks for.)"),
        ], check=r"\frac{-20/70}{100/550} = -\frac{20 \times 550}{70 \times 100} = -1.571"),
        H("Determinants of PED"),
        KEY("What makes demand more or less elastic?", "", cards=[
            ["Availability of substitutes", "Many close substitutes make demand elastic (one brand of soap); few make it inelastic (salt, electricity)."],
            ["Necessity or luxury", "Necessities have inelastic demand (basic food, medicine); luxuries have elastic demand (jewellery, holidays)."],
            ["Share of income spent", "A small share (matches) gives inelastic demand; a large share (a car) gives elastic demand."],
            ["Time", "Over a longer period people find substitutes, so demand is more elastic."],
            ["Habit or addiction", "Goods that people are used to buying have inelastic demand."],
            ["How broadly the good is defined", "Food in general is inelastic; one brand of biscuit is elastic."],
        ]),
        H("PED and total revenue"),
        D("**Total revenue** is the price multiplied by the quantity sold: TR = P x Q.", title="Total revenue"),
        KEY("What happens to revenue if the firm raises price?", "", cards=[
            ["Demand elastic", "Quantity falls by more than the rise in price: total revenue falls."],
            ["Demand inelastic", "Quantity falls by less than the rise in price: total revenue rises."],
            ["Unit elastic", "Total revenue does not change."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: revenue and elasticity", intro="Cooking oil: at FCFA 500 and 80 litres, then FCFA 600 and 60 litres. Show how total revenue changes. A second case: salt at FCFA 200 and 100 packets, then FCFA 250 and 95 packets.", steps=[
            S("Oil: before and after", math=r"500 \times 80 = 40\,000 \quad ; \quad 600 \times 60 = 36\,000", text="Revenue falls, which matches elastic demand."),
            S("Salt: PED", math=r"\frac{-5\%}{+25\%} = -0.2", text="Inelastic."),
            S("Salt: revenue", math=r"200 \times 100 = 20\,000 \quad ; \quad 250 \times 95 = 23\,750", text="Revenue rises, which matches inelastic demand."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Dividing price change by quantity change", "PED = % change in **quantity** divided by % change in **price**. Quantity goes on top."),
        MISTAKE("Saying a steep curve always has the same elasticity everywhere", "Along a straight-line demand curve elasticity changes: it is high at high prices and low at low prices. A steep curve is only **relatively** inelastic. The point elasticity of Qd = 120 - 0.1P is 0.2 at FCFA 200, 1 at FCFA 600 and 5 at FCFA 1 000."),
        QUIZ("A 10% rise in price leads to a 15% fall in quantity demanded. PED is:",
             ["-0.67, inelastic", "-1.5, elastic", "-1.5, inelastic", "-25, elastic"], 1,
             "-15 / 10 = -1.5. Its size is greater than 1, so demand is elastic.",
             hint="Put the quantity change on top.",
             feedback=["That is the reverse (10/15).", "", "A size above 1 means elastic.", "You added the percentages."]),
        QUIZ("For which good is demand likely to be most inelastic?",
             ["A particular brand of lemonade", "Salt", "Foreign holidays", "A new car"], 1,
             "Salt is a cheap necessity with no close substitute, so demand is inelastic.",
             hint="Think of necessity, cost and substitutes.",
             feedback=["There are many substitute brands.", "", "A luxury with substitutes (and large cost).", "Large share of income, so elastic."]),
        TIP("Exam link: the Board's 2023 question analysis names the determinants of PED, so expect a part asking you to list and explain them, and calculation questions. Show the formula, the substitution and the sign."),
        PQ("Price rises from FCFA 100 to FCFA 120 and quantity demanded falls from 500 to 450. Calculate PED by the percentage method and state the type.", "% change in price = 20/100 = +20%. % change in quantity = -50/500 = -10%. PED = -10/20 = -0.5. Inelastic."),
        PQ("Use the midpoint method for the data in the previous question.", "Average Q = 475, average P = 110. % change Q = -50/475 = -10.53%. % change P = 20/110 = 18.18%. PED = -0.58."),
        PQ("State four determinants of price elasticity of demand.", "Availability of substitutes; necessity or luxury; proportion of income spent; time period; habit. (Any four.)"),
        PQ("A trader's demand is inelastic. Should she raise or lower her price to increase total revenue? Explain.", "Raise it. When demand is inelastic the quantity falls by a smaller percentage than the price rises, so total revenue increases."),
        SUM("PED = % change in quantity demanded / % change in price. Ignore the sign when classifying.",
            "Greater than 1 is elastic, less than 1 inelastic, equal to 1 unitary.",
            "The midpoint method uses averages as the base and gives the same answer in both directions.",
            "Determinants: substitutes, necessity, share of income, time, habit. Elastic demand: price rise cuts revenue. Inelastic: price rise increases revenue."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 17. PES, YED, XED
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Elasticity",
    title="Elasticity of supply, income elasticity and cross elasticity",
    slug="eco1-pes-yed-xed",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Price elasticity of supply, income elasticity of demand and cross elasticity of demand use the same percentage idea. Learn the formulas, the meaning of the signs, the determinants of PES and how to interpret answers.",
    objectives=[
        "Calculate price elasticity of supply and state its determinants",
        "Calculate income elasticity of demand and classify normal, luxury and inferior goods",
        "Calculate cross elasticity of demand and identify substitutes and complements",
        "Interpret the sign and size of each elasticity",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Price elasticity of supply (PES)", "Income elasticity of demand (YED)", "Cross elasticity of demand (XED)", "Inferior good", "Substitutes and complements"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Elasticity is a general idea: it compares the percentage change in one thing with the percentage change in another. After price elasticity of demand we meet three more types. "
          "The Board's 2023 report names the determinants of price elasticity of supply in its Price and Market question, the question most candidates failed, so study PES carefully."),
        H("Price elasticity of supply (PES)"),
        D("**Price elasticity of supply** measures how much the quantity supplied changes when the price changes.", title="Definition: PES", formula=r"PES = \frac{\%\ \text{change in quantity supplied}}{\%\ \text{change in price}}"),
        T("PES is normally positive because supply slopes up. If it is greater than 1, supply is **elastic**; if between 0 and 1, **inelastic**; if equal to 1, unitary."),
        EX("Worked example 1: PES", intro="The price of tomatoes rises from FCFA 400 to FCFA 500 a basket and the quantity supplied rises from 40 to 60 baskets (invented for practice). Calculate PES by the percentage and midpoint methods.", steps=[
            S("% change in price", math=r"\frac{100}{400} \times 100 = 25\%"),
            S("% change in quantity", math=r"\frac{20}{40} \times 100 = 50\%"),
            S("Percentage method", math=r"PES = \frac{50}{25} = 2", text="Elastic."),
            S("Midpoint method", math=r"\frac{20/50}{100/450} = \frac{0.4}{0.2222} = 1.8", text="Also elastic."),
        ]),
        KEY("Determinants of PES", "How easily can producers increase output when the price rises?", cards=[
            ["Time", "In the short run supply is inelastic because firms cannot quickly change inputs. In the long run it is more elastic."],
            ["Spare capacity", "Firms with idle machines or workers can raise output quickly."],
            ["Stocks of finished goods", "If goods can be taken from stock, supply responds quickly."],
            ["Ease of storing the product", "Non-perishable goods can be stored and released when prices rise."],
            ["Availability and mobility of factors", "Skilled labour, raw materials and land that can be moved to the product make supply elastic."],
            ["Nature of the product", "Farm crops that take a season to grow have inelastic supply in the short run; manufactured goods are usually more elastic. Tree crops such as cocoa take years."],
        ]),
        H("Income elasticity of demand (YED)"),
        D("**Income elasticity of demand** measures how much the demand for a good changes when consumers' income changes.", title="Definition: YED", formula=r"YED = \frac{\%\ \text{change in quantity demanded}}{\%\ \text{change in income}}"),
        KEY("Interpreting YED", "", cards=[
            ["Positive and greater than 1", "A **luxury** (income-elastic) good: demand rises faster than income (a car, holidays)."],
            ["Positive and less than 1", "A **normal necessity**: demand rises more slowly than income (staple food)."],
            ["Negative", "An **inferior good**: demand falls when income rises (cheap second-hand clothes, low-grade foods)."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: YED", intro="A household's monthly income rises from FCFA 100 000 to FCFA 120 000 (invented for practice). Its monthly purchases of beef rise from 10 kg to 13 kg and of a cheap cereal fall from 10 kg to 9 kg. Find YED for each.", steps=[
            S("% change in income", math=r"\frac{20\,000}{100\,000} \times 100 = 20\%"),
            S("Beef", math=r"\frac{+3}{10} = +30\% \Rightarrow YED = \frac{30}{20} = +1.5", text="Positive and greater than 1: a luxury (income-elastic) good."),
            S("Cheap cereal", math=r"\frac{-1}{10} = -10\% \Rightarrow YED = \frac{-10}{20} = -0.5", text="Negative: an inferior good."),
        ]),
        H("Cross elasticity of demand (XED)"),
        D("**Cross elasticity of demand** measures how much the demand for one good (A) changes when the price of another good (B) changes.", title="Definition: XED", formula=r"XED = \frac{\%\ \text{change in quantity demanded of A}}{\%\ \text{change in price of B}}"),
        KEY("Interpreting XED", "", cards=[
            ["Positive", "A and B are **substitutes**: a dearer B raises demand for A (tea and coffee)."],
            ["Negative", "A and B are **complements**: a dearer B lowers demand for A (cars and petrol)."],
            ["Close to zero", "The goods are **unrelated**."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 3: XED", intro="The price of tea rises by 10% and the quantity demanded of coffee rises by 5%. When the price of cars rises by 10% the demand for petrol falls by 4% (invented for practice). Find XED in each case.", steps=[
            S("Tea and coffee", math=r"XED = \frac{+5}{+10} = +0.5", text="Positive: substitutes."),
            S("Cars and petrol", math=r"XED = \frac{-4}{+10} = -0.4", text="Negative: complements."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Dropping the sign in YED and XED", "In PED we may ignore the sign, but for YED and XED the **sign carries the meaning**: negative YED = inferior good; positive XED = substitutes, negative XED = complements."),
        MISTAKE("Using the wrong change as the divisor", "PES divides by the change in the good's own price; YED divides by the change in income; XED divides by the change in the price of the **other** good."),
        QUIZ("The income elasticity of demand for a good is -0.5. The good is:",
             ["A luxury", "A normal good", "An inferior good", "A complement"], 2,
             "A negative YED means demand falls when income rises, so it is an inferior good.",
             hint="What does a negative sign mean?",
             feedback=["A luxury has YED above 1.", "A normal good has positive YED.", "", "Complements are identified by XED, not YED."]),
        QUIZ("Which determinant makes supply more elastic?",
             ["Little spare capacity", "Short time period", "Perishable goods that cannot be stored", "Large stocks of finished goods"], 3,
             "If firms hold stocks they can quickly put more goods on the market when the price rises.",
             hint="Which one lets output rise quickly?",
             feedback=["Spare capacity makes supply elastic; a lack of it makes supply inelastic.", "A short time makes supply inelastic.", "Goods that cannot be stored make supply inelastic.", ""]),
        TIP("Exam link: the Board's 2023 analysis names price elasticity of supply determinants; expect a list-and-explain question and calculations. State the formula first, substitute the numbers and then interpret."),
        PQ("Price rises from FCFA 50 to FCFA 60 and the quantity supplied rises from 200 to 230. Calculate PES.", "% change in price = 10/50 = 20%. % change in quantity = 30/200 = 15%. PES = 15/20 = 0.75: inelastic."),
        PQ("Income rises by 8% and demand for restaurant meals rises by 12%. Calculate YED and say what kind of good this is.", "YED = 12/8 = 1.5. Greater than 1: a luxury (income-elastic) good."),
        PQ("The price of rice rises by 20% and the quantity demanded of maize flour rises by 10%. Calculate XED and interpret it.", "XED = 10/20 = +0.5. Positive: the goods are substitutes."),
        SUM("PES = % change in quantity supplied / % change in price; it is usually positive.",
            "Determinants of PES: time, spare capacity, stocks, storage, mobility of factors, nature of product.",
            "YED positive above 1 = luxury, positive below 1 = normal necessity, negative = inferior.",
            "XED positive = substitutes, negative = complements, near zero = unrelated."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 18. Price controls
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Price controls",
    title="Price ceilings and price floors",
    slug="eco1-price-ceilings-floors",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Governments sometimes control prices. A maximum price (ceiling) below equilibrium causes a shortage; a minimum price (floor) above equilibrium causes a surplus. Learn the diagrams, effects and examples.",
    objectives=[
        "Define a price ceiling (maximum price) and a price floor (minimum price)",
        "Show each on a demand and supply diagram and identify the shortage or surplus",
        "Explain the effects, including black markets, rationing and storage costs",
        "Evaluate why governments use price controls",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Price ceiling", "Price floor", "Shortage", "Surplus", "Black market", "Minimum wage", "Rationing"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("When the price of a basic good such as cooking oil, rice or fuel rises sharply, many governments are tempted to fix a maximum price to protect consumers. "
          "When crop prices fall very low, they may fix a minimum price to protect farmers. Price controls are an example of **government intervention** in the market."),
        D("A **price ceiling (maximum price)** is a legal limit above which the price of a good cannot be charged. To work, it must be set **below** the equilibrium price.", title="Definition: price ceiling"),
        D("A **price floor (minimum price)** is a legal limit below which the price of a good cannot be sold. To work, it must be set **above** the equilibrium price.", title="Definition: price floor"),
        T("In all the diagrams in this lesson the market is the one from the earlier lessons (**invented for practice**): Qd = 120 - 0.1P and Qs = 0.1P, with an equilibrium of FCFA 600 and 60 bunches."),
        H("Price ceiling"),
        IMG("f5eco-1-ceiling.svg", alt="Demand and supply with equilibrium at price 600; a horizontal maximum price line at 400 gives quantity supplied 40 and quantity demanded 80, a shortage of 40",
            caption="Figure 1. A ceiling at FCFA 400, below the equilibrium of FCFA 600, creates a shortage of 40 bunches."),
        EX("Worked example 1: the shortage under a ceiling", intro="A maximum price of FCFA 400 per bunch is set. Find the quantity demanded, the quantity supplied and the shortage.", steps=[
            S("Quantity demanded at 400", math=r"120 - 0.1(400) = 80"),
            S("Quantity supplied at 400", math=r"0.1(400) = 40"),
            S("Shortage", math=r"80 - 40 = 40", text="Only 40 bunches reach the market, although buyers want 80."),
        ]),
        KEY("Effects of a price ceiling", "", cards=[
            ["Shortage", "Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied."],
            ["Rationing and queues", "Goods are shared by first come first served, by favour or by coupons."],
            ["Black (illegal) markets", "Some goods are sold secretly at prices above the ceiling."],
            ["Lower quality and less supply", "Producers earn less and may cut quality or leave the market."],
            ["Benefit", "Consumers who do manage to buy pay less."],
        ]),
        T("Governments use ceilings to keep essentials affordable for poor households, for example on basic foods, rent or transport fares. The alternatives include subsidies and increasing supply. "
          "Cameroon has, from time to time, set or negotiated limits on the prices of some basic goods and fuel; check current rules, since they change."),
        H("Price floor"),
        IMG("f5eco-1-floor.svg", alt="Demand and supply with equilibrium at price 600; a horizontal minimum price line at 800 gives quantity demanded 40 and quantity supplied 80, a surplus of 40",
            caption="Figure 2. A floor at FCFA 800, above the equilibrium of FCFA 600, creates a surplus of 40 bunches."),
        EX("Worked example 2: the surplus and cost of a floor", intro="A minimum price of FCFA 800 is set. Find the surplus. If the government buys all the surplus at the minimum price, what does it spend?", steps=[
            S("Quantity demanded at 800", math=r"120 - 0.1(800) = 40"),
            S("Quantity supplied at 800", math=r"0.1(800) = 80"),
            S("Surplus", math=r"80 - 40 = 40"),
            S("Cost to the government", math=r"40 \times 800 = 32\,000 \text{ FCFA}", text="This money must come from taxes, and the stored surplus may rot."),
        ]),
        KEY("Effects of a price floor", "", cards=[
            ["Surplus", "Quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded."],
            ["Storage or destruction", "Someone has to buy, store, export or destroy the surplus."],
            ["Higher incomes for sellers who sell", "Farmers who sell at the floor price gain."],
            ["Higher prices for consumers", "Buyers pay more and buy less."],
            ["Encourages overproduction", "Producers grow more than the market needs."],
        ]),
        T("A minimum price for crops protects farmers' incomes. A **minimum wage** is a price floor in the labour market. If it is above the equilibrium wage, the supply of workers exceeds the demand for workers, which can cause unemployment."),
        MISTAKE("Setting the ceiling above the equilibrium price", "A ceiling above equilibrium has no effect because the market price is already below it. To cause a shortage it must be **below** equilibrium. Similarly a floor must be **above** equilibrium."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up shortage and surplus", "Ceiling gives a **shortage**; floor gives a **surplus**. Mark the shortage or surplus as a horizontal gap between the demand and supply curves at the controlled price."),
        QUIZ("A government sets a maximum price below equilibrium. The result is:",
             ["A surplus", "A shortage", "No effect", "Higher quantity supplied"], 1,
             "At the lower price quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied.",
             hint="Which is larger at a low price?",
             feedback=["A surplus follows a minimum price above equilibrium.", "", "It has an effect because it is below equilibrium.", "A lower price reduces quantity supplied."]),
        QUIZ("Which is a likely effect of a minimum price for maize set above equilibrium?",
             ["A shortage of maize", "A surplus of maize that must be stored or bought", "A fall in the price below equilibrium", "A rise in demand"], 1,
             "At the higher price supply exceeds demand, creating a surplus.",
             hint="Is the quantity supplied larger or smaller than quantity demanded at a higher price?",
             feedback=["A shortage follows a ceiling.", "", "The floor prevents that.", "Demand falls along the curve; the curve does not shift."]),
        TIP("Exam link: price controls are examined in Paper 1 and as a Paper 2 part on prices and markets. Draw the diagram, mark the controlled price and the shortage or surplus, and explain the effects."),
        PQ("Define a price floor and give one example.", "A price floor is a legal minimum price set above equilibrium, for example a minimum price for a farm crop or a minimum wage."),
        PQ("Equilibrium price is FCFA 50 and quantity 100. A ceiling of FCFA 30 gives Qd = 140 and Qs = 60 (invented). Find the shortage.", "Shortage = 140 - 60 = 80 units."),
        PQ("Explain two problems caused by a maximum price.", "Shortages and queues, because demand exceeds supply; black markets selling above the legal price; lower quality or suppliers leaving. (Any two.)"),
        SUM("A ceiling is a maximum price set below equilibrium; it causes a shortage.",
            "A floor is a minimum price set above equilibrium; it causes a surplus.",
            "Ceilings protect consumers but cause queues, rationing and black markets; floors protect producers but need storage or purchase of surplus.",
            "A minimum wage is a price floor in the labour market."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 19. Taxes and subsidies
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M3, topic="Taxes and subsidies",
    title="Taxes, subsidies and who bears the burden",
    slug="eco1-taxes-subsidies-incidence",
    minutes=25,
    summary="An indirect tax raises supply costs, shifts the supply curve up and is shared between buyers and sellers. A subsidy lowers costs and shifts supply down. Learn incidence, calculations and how elasticity decides the burden.",
    objectives=[
        "Show the effect of a per-unit tax and a subsidy on a market diagram",
        "Calculate the new equilibrium, the tax revenue and the share of the tax borne by buyers and sellers",
        "Explain how elasticity decides who bears more of the tax",
        "State the reasons for and disadvantages of taxes and subsidies",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Per-unit tax", "Incidence of tax", "Subsidy", "Elasticity and burden", "Tax revenue"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("When the government puts a tax on every litre of fuel or every bottle of beer, the seller pays the tax to the state, but who really pays? Part of it is passed on to buyers in higher prices, and part is paid by the seller through lower profit. "
          "The **incidence** of a tax tells us who bears the burden."),
        D("A **per-unit (specific) tax** is a fixed amount charged on each unit sold. It raises the cost of supplying each unit, so the **supply curve shifts upward (left)** by the amount of the tax.", title="Definition: per-unit tax"),
        D("The **incidence of a tax** is the way the burden of the tax is shared between buyers and sellers.", title="Definition: incidence"),
        T("Our market (**invented for practice**) has demand Qd = 120 - 0.1P and a flatter supply Qs = 0.3P - 120. Equilibrium is found by setting them equal: 120 - 0.1P = 0.3P - 120 gives P = 600 and Q = 60."),
        IMG("f5eco-1-tax.svg", alt="Demand D and supply S with equilibrium E0 at price 600 and quantity 60; a tax of 100 shifts supply up to S plus tax, giving E1 at price 675 and quantity 52.5, with buyers paying 675 and sellers keeping 575",
            caption="Figure 1. A tax of FCFA 100 a bunch shifts supply up. Buyers now pay FCFA 675 and sellers keep FCFA 575 after paying the tax."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a tax of FCFA 100 per bunch", intro="Find the new price paid by buyers, the price kept by sellers, the new quantity, the tax revenue and the burden of each side.", steps=[
            S("New supply (sellers receive P - 100)", math=r"Q_s = 0.3(P - 100) - 120 = 0.3P - 150"),
            S("Set equal to demand", math=r"120 - 0.1P = 0.3P - 150 \Rightarrow 270 = 0.4P \Rightarrow P = 675"),
            S("New quantity", math=r"Q = 120 - 0.1(675) = 52.5"),
            S("What sellers keep", math=r"675 - 100 = 575"),
            S("Tax revenue", math=r"100 \times 52.5 = 5250 \text{ FCFA}"),
            S("Burden", math=r"\text{Buyers: } 675 - 600 = 75 \; ; \; \text{Sellers: } 600 - 575 = 25", text="Buyers bear 75% of the tax (75 out of 100) and sellers 25%, because buyers' demand is less responsive than sellers' supply."),
        ], check=r"75 + 25 = 100"),
        KEY("Who bears more of the tax?", "The side that is **less elastic** (less able to avoid the tax) bears more.", cards=[
            ["Demand inelastic", "Buyers need the good and keep buying, so they pay most of the tax (fuel, salt, cigarettes)."],
            ["Demand elastic", "Buyers walk away if the price rises, so sellers must absorb most of the tax."],
            ["Supply inelastic", "Sellers cannot easily cut output, so they bear more of the tax."],
        ]),
        T("This is why governments often tax goods with inelastic demand (fuel, alcohol and tobacco): the quantity falls little and revenue is high."),
        H("Subsidies"),
        D("A **subsidy** is a payment by the government to producers (or consumers) that lowers the cost of production or the price paid. A per-unit subsidy shifts the **supply curve down (right)** by the amount of the subsidy.", title="Definition: subsidy"),
        IMG("f5eco-1-subsidy.svg", alt="Demand D and supply S with equilibrium at price 600 and quantity 60; a subsidy of 100 shifts supply down to S plus subsidy and gives a new equilibrium at price 550 and quantity 65",
            caption="Figure 2. A subsidy of FCFA 100 a bunch shifts supply down. Buyers pay FCFA 550 and sellers receive FCFA 650."),
        EX("Worked example 2: a subsidy of FCFA 100 per bunch", intro="Using the first market (Qd = 120 - 0.1P, Qs = 0.1P), find the new price, quantity and cost of the subsidy.", steps=[
            S("New supply (sellers get P + 100)", math=r"Q_s = 0.1(P + 100) = 0.1P + 10"),
            S("Set equal to demand", math=r"120 - 0.1P = 0.1P + 10 \Rightarrow 110 = 0.2P \Rightarrow P = 550"),
            S("Quantity", math=r"Q = 120 - 0.1(550) = 65"),
            S("What sellers receive", math=r"550 + 100 = 650"),
            S("Cost to the government", math=r"100 \times 65 = 6500 \text{ FCFA}", text="Buyers gain FCFA 50 per bunch (600 to 550) and sellers gain FCFA 50 (600 to 650): the subsidy is shared."),
        ], check=r"0.1(550) + 10 = 65"),
        KEY("Why governments use taxes and subsidies", "", cards=[
            ["Taxes: raise revenue", "To pay for public services."],
            ["Taxes: discourage harmful goods", "Alcohol, tobacco and pollution."],
            ["Subsidies: lower prices of essentials", "Basic foods, fertiliser, health."],
            ["Subsidies: support farmers and new industries", "Raise output and protect jobs."],
        ]),
        T("**Disadvantages:** taxes reduce output and may lead to smuggling; subsidies cost the government money, may encourage inefficiency and are hard to remove once started."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking the seller pays the whole tax because he pays it to the government", "The legal payer is not always the economic payer. Incidence depends on elasticity: buyers can bear part or most of the tax through a higher price."),
        MISTAKE("Shifting the demand curve for a tax on producers", "A per-unit tax on sellers shifts the **supply** curve (up, or left). Demand does not shift."),
        QUIZ("Which good is a government most likely to tax heavily to raise revenue?",
             ["A good with elastic demand", "A good with inelastic demand", "A free good", "A good with many substitutes"], 1,
             "When demand is inelastic, the quantity falls only slightly, so revenue stays high.",
             hint="Which good do people keep buying even if the price rises?",
             feedback=["Buyers would switch away and revenue would be small.", "", "Free goods have no price to tax.", "Buyers would switch to substitutes."]),
        QUIZ("A subsidy to producers shifts the supply curve:",
             ["Left (decrease in supply)", "Right (increase in supply)", "Not at all", "Upwards and leftwards"], 1,
             "A subsidy lowers costs, so supply increases at every price.",
             hint="Does a subsidy raise or lower costs?",
             feedback=["That is the effect of a tax.", "", "It changes the cost of supplying.", "That is the effect of a tax."]),
        TIP("Exam link: taxes, subsidies and their effects appear in Paper 1 and in the Paper 2 questions on price and on public finance. Always say which curve shifts, in which direction, and what happens to price and quantity."),
        PQ("A tax of FCFA 100 raises the price paid by buyers from 600 to 675. How much of the tax do buyers bear, and what is the share in percent?", "Buyers bear 75 of the 100 (75%). The remaining 25 is borne by sellers."),
        PQ("Explain why a tax on a good with inelastic demand falls mostly on the buyers.", "With inelastic demand buyers continue to buy even at a higher price, so sellers can pass most of the tax on in the price."),
        PQ("State two reasons why a government may subsidise farmers and one disadvantage of subsidies.", "To lower food prices and raise farm output and incomes. Disadvantage: it costs the government money and may make farmers inefficient."),
        SUM("A per-unit tax shifts supply up (left); the new price is higher and the quantity lower.",
            "The incidence is shared between buyers and sellers; the less elastic side bears more.",
            "A subsidy shifts supply down (right): price falls and quantity rises.",
            "Tax revenue = tax per unit x new quantity; subsidy cost = subsidy per unit x new quantity."),
    ],
))
